{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2752 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "9269483f44ff445ad514813c725bd0bfbafeee92f769177b00b5160594b2f1d4" :edb/db-before-t 2751 :edb/db-after-t 2752 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536064 50 #inst "2026-10-15T23:46:29.617Z" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186054604 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563660 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186054604 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563742 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186054658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563759 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186054703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563676 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186064268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563733 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186064323 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563684 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186064348 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563666 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186068701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563688 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186073752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563725 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186075298 1078 #edb/ref 17592186075301 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186078393 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563724 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186078394 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563680 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186078532 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563669 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186078737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186078737 1071 "![](images/d0/d04a8e993da8e90ca75f5f7541317d26a6a9c8f9b76cdb9c95511080035177c5.png)\n\nFind the cosine of $∠Y$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186078737 1071 "![](images/d0/d04a8e993da8e90ca75f5f7541317d26a6a9c8f9b76cdb9c95511080035177c5.png)\n\nFind the cosine of $∠Y.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186078737 1074 "Recall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡Y=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{XY}{YZ}=\\frac{XY}{5}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $XY.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{Y{Z}^{2}-X{Z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25-7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡Y & =\\frac{XY}{5}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186083166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563677 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186083167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563681 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186083185 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563708 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186086328 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086329 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186086328 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086330 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186086328 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086332 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186086328 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086333 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186086328 1075 #edb/ref 17592186562968 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186086328 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563118 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186086328 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563209 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186086328 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563355 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186086328 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563118 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186087851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563673 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186087851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563698 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186087852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563717 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186087853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563670 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186088279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186088279 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = (7x^{2} + 4)/(x(3x^{2} + 12))$." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186088279 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{2}+4}{x(3{x}^{2}+12)}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186088279 1074 "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{2}+4}{3{x}^{3}+12x}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7{x}^{2}+4}{3{x}^{3}+12x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{12x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})}{(3+\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{x},\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})}{(3+\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{3+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186094843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094844 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186094843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094845 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186094843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094846 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186094843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094847 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186094843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094848 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186094843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186562983 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186094843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563251 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186094843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563277 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186094843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563405 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186094843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563499 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186094843 1078 #edb/ref 17592186562983 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186096784 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563726 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186096785 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563694 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186097145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186097145 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.2p=0.6 \\\\ -p-5q=7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186097145 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n${0.2p = 0.6; - p - 5q = 7$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186097145 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the top equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.2p=0.6 & |\\,\\times 10 \\\\ -p-5q=7 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2p=6 \\\\ -p-5q=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can easily solve for $p$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p & =6 \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the value of $q,$ we can substitute $p=3$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-p-5q & =7 \\\\ -3-5q & =7 \\\\ -5q & =10 \\\\ q & =\\frac{10}{-5} \\\\ q & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=3,\\,q=-2.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186107606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563182 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186107606 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563182 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186109039 1078 #edb/ref 17592186109041 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110018 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110019 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110020 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110021 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110022 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563062 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563208 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563236 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563278 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110017 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563377 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110017 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563377 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110302 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y = \\log (x)$?\n\n![](images/5f/5f38ded6da0c01657574cf0a40d86d2c5d2ce6b9ff1c29ceaaefbbe82103a277.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110302 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y = \\log (x)?$\n\n![](images/5f/5f38ded6da0c01657574cf0a40d86d2c5d2ce6b9ff1c29ceaaefbbe82103a277.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110302 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\log (x)$:\n\n- The graph passes through the point $(1,0)$.\n- For $x>1$, the function is positive and grows very slowly as we increase $x$. We say that $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ (slowly) as $x\\rightarrow \\infty$.\n- The domain is $x>0$, and the range is $-\\infty < y < \\infty$. The function is not defined at $x=0$.\n- The function has the vertical asymptote $x=0$.\n- The function is an increasing function.\n\nOf the given plots, the only one that displays all of these properties is II. (For example, plots I, III, and IV all do not have a vertical asymptote $x=0$.)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110302 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\log (x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- The graph passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\n- For $x>1,$ the function is positive and grows very slowly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ (slowly) as $x\\rightarrow \\infty.$\n\n- The domain is $x>0$, and the range is $-\\infty < y < \\infty.$ The function is not defined at $x=0.$\n\n- The function has the vertical asymptote $x=0.$\n\n- The function is an increasing function.\n\nOf the given plots, the only one that displays all of these properties is II. (For example, plots I, III, and IV all do not have a vertical asymptote $x=0.$)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110303 1071 "What are the domain and range of the function $y=\\ln x ?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110303 1071 "What are the domain and range of the function $y=\\ln x$?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110303 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y = \\ln x$.\n\n![](images/00/004c1050017c1a0631d0dda46c8335248b4f05dad15645513b71e88588d4ed0c.png)\n\nThe domain of $y=\\ln x$ is $x \\in (0,\\infty)$ and the range is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110303 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y = \\ln x.$\n\n![](images/00/004c1050017c1a0631d0dda46c8335248b4f05dad15645513b71e88588d4ed0c.png)\n\nThe domain of $y=\\ln x$ is $x \\in (0,\\infty)$ and the range is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110304 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\ln(x)$?\n\n1. $y\\rightarrow-\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow 0^+$\n2. The curve passes through the point $(1,0)$\n3. $y\\rightarrow\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110304 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\ln(x)?$\n\nI. $y\\rightarrow-\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow 0^+$\n\nII. The curve passes through the point $(1,0)$\n\nIII. $y\\rightarrow\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110304 1074 "First, let's plot the graph of $y=\\ln (x)$.\n\n![](images/d9/d9ca282050c64ffc1ab1950dd6d89288e6d65d680e45bbc9ef29c1a4ae2c3a67.png)\n\nNow, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $0^+$, $y$ decreases without bound.\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve gives a result of $0$:\n$y|= \\ln (1) = 0✓$\n- Statement III is false. The function is not defined for $x<0$, so the function is not defined as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110304 1074 "First, let's plot the graph of $y=\\ln (x).$\n\n![](images/d9/d9ca282050c64ffc1ab1950dd6d89288e6d65d680e45bbc9ef29c1a4ae2c3a67.png)\n\nNow, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $0^+,$ $y$ decreases without bound.\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve gives a result of $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not defined for $x<0,$ so the function is not defined as $x \\to -\\infty.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110305 1071 "What is the vertical asymptote of $y=\\log_{2} x$?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110305 1071 "What is the vertical asymptote of $y=\\log_{2} x?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110305 1074 "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\log_{2} x\n$$\n\n![](images/96/96d7da4dd7415b769803b9cea8ebf91b65db2ba5afe0249af9d16521eb0f4818.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function\n\n$$\ny=\\log_{2} x\n$$\n\nhas the vertical asymptote at $x=0$." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110305 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\log_{2} x.$\n\n![](images/96/96d7da4dd7415b769803b9cea8ebf91b65db2ba5afe0249af9d16521eb0f4818.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function $y=\\log_{2} x$ has the vertical asymptote at $x=0.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563695 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563710 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563745 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563747 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563715 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110725 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563701 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110725 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563721 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110725 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563762 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563686 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563705 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563735 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563746 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563646 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563653 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563685 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563697 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563654 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563659 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563728 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563739 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563740 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110877 1050 420.0 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110978 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y = - (x + 5k)(3x - 4k)$ is $x =-\\frac{1}{8}$. Find the value of $k$.$k =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186110978 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=-(x+5k)(3x-4k)$ is $x=-\\frac{1}{8}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186110978 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{8},$ we will set our result equal to $\\frac{1}{8}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=-(x+5k)(3x-4k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-5k$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{4k}{3}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-5k+\\frac{4k}{3})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-\\frac{11k}{3})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11k}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{8}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{11k}{6} & =-\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ -11k & =-\\frac{6}{8} \\\\ -11k & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ k & =\\frac{3}{44}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186111219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186111219 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\log x$?\n\n1. The function passes through the point $(1, 0)$\n2. Its horizontal asymptote is $y = 0$.\n3. It passes through the point $(10, 100)$." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186111219 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡x?$\n\nI. The function passes through the point $(1,0)$\n\nII. Its horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$\n\nIII. It passes through the point $(10,100).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186111219 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/17/177d2949118515d96b6a1c49d0222a2417ab6236e8641f8d89c0cd8f6b30a592.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. The function $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡x$ has no horizontal asymptote. However, it does have the vertical asymptote $x=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Substituting $x=10$ into the equation of the curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(10) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1}) \\\\  & =1\\ne 100\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186111382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186111382 1071 "Which of the following is a zero of $y = \\log_{5} x$?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186111382 1071 "Which of the following is a zero of $y={log}_{5}⁡x?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186111382 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{5}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f0/f095d09fd04a982ad51ff3859f6d789083ed7528b382457954e45cf259cc06a5.png)\n\nNote that the graph passes through the point $(1,0).$ \n\nTherefore, the zero of the function $y={log}_{5}⁡x$ is $x=1.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186111907 1064 "To plot the graph of a logarithmic function, it helps to remember that the inverse of a logarithmic function is an exponential function. So, we can just plot the exponential function and reflect it over the line $y=x$.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\log_2 x\n$$\n\nThe corresponding inverse function is $y=2^x$, and since $y=2^x$ is a one-to-one function defined for all real $x$, we can obtain the graph of $y=\\log_2{x}$ by reflecting $y=2^x$ over the line $y=x$. The result is shown below:\n\n![](images/0c/0c614201798324262429f080d8e8a31bfefe58c4008bfe82e6da1b91dc158b7a.png)\n\nHere are some key points to note about\n\n$$\ny=\\log_2(x)\n$$\n\n- The domain is $x>0$, and the range is $-\\infty < y < \\infty$.\n- The graph has the vertical asymptote $x=0$.\n- The function is an increasing function.\n- The graph passes through the point $(1,0)$.\n- For $x>1$, the graph is positive and grows very slowly as we increase $x$. We say that $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ (slowly) as $x\\rightarrow \\infty$.\n\nIn general, for any base $n>1$, the graph of $y=\\log_n{x}$ always has the same shape, and all of the above points are valid. We can verify that this is true by inspecting some possible graphs shown below:\n\n![](images/91/91567f282acbc3b8390da1e4147b445018ae649d913205e59867d58ea980ff7b.png)\n\n**Note:** The important points covered above also apply to two familiar logarithms.\n\n- $y = \\ln x = \\log_e x$ is the natural logarithm. Since the base is $n=e>1$, all of the important points covered above apply.\n- $y = \\log x = \\log_{10} x$ is the logarithm with base $10$. Since the base is $n=10>1$, all the important points covered above apply." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186111907 1064 "To plot the graph of a logarithmic function, it helps to remember that the inverse of a logarithmic function is an exponential function. So, we can just plot the exponential function and reflect it over the line $y=x.$\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to plot the graph of \n\n$$\ny=\\log_2 x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding inverse function is $y=2^x,$ and since $y=2^x$ is a one-to-one function defined for all real $x,$ we can obtain the graph of $y=\\log_2{x}$ by reflecting $y=2^x$ over the line $y=x.$ The result is shown below:\n\n![](images/0c/0c614201798324262429f080d8e8a31bfefe58c4008bfe82e6da1b91dc158b7a.png)\n\nHere are some key points to note about $y=\\log_2(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- The domain is $x>0$, and the range is $-\\infty <   y <   \\infty.$\n\n- The graph has the vertical asymptote $x=0.$\n\n- The function is an increasing function.\n\n- The graph passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\n- For $x>1$, the graph is positive and grows very slowly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ (slowly) as $x\\rightarrow \\infty.$\n\nIn general, for any base $n>1,$ the graph of $y=\\log_n{x}$ always has the same shape, and all of the above points are valid. We can verify that this is true by inspecting some possible graphs shown below:\n\n![](images/91/91567f282acbc3b8390da1e4147b445018ae649d913205e59867d58ea980ff7b.png)\n\n**Note:** The important points covered above also apply to two familiar logarithms.\n\n- $y = \\ln x = \\log_e x$ is the natural logarithm. Since the base is $n=e>1,$ all of the important points covered above apply.\n\n- $y = \\log x = \\log_{10} x$ is the logarithm with base $10.$ Since the base is $n=10>1,$ all the important points covered above apply." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113010 1078 #edb/ref 17592186113012 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113508 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113509 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186113508 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113510 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186113508 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113511 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186113508 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113512 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186113508 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113513 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186113508 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563061 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113508 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563070 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113508 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563111 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113508 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563180 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113508 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563369 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113508 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563070 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113520 1078 #edb/ref 17592186113523 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113532 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113534 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186113532 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563390 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113532 1078 #edb/ref 17592186113536 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113594 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186113592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113597 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186113592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563043 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563302 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113592 1078 #edb/ref 17