rue] [17592186113767 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563386 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113767 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563471 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186113767 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563303 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186114133 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556707 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186114133 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563504 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115105 1071 "Plot the curve of $y=\\log_7 (x+4)$." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186115105 1071 "Plot the curve of $y=\\log_7 (x+4).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115105 1074 "To plot\n\n$$\ny=\\log_7 {(x+4)}\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\log_7 {x}$.\n\n![](images/97/975f58b320e2293dbfaf267a3acfe9187438ae40add3603bac98d142b35c44c1.png)\n\n- Translate it by $4$ units to the left to get the graph of $y=\\log_7 {(x+4)}$.\n\n![](images/ce/ce5db8eb1adb7e84337427c1c769eabec9cb420b51a7e867f47e8d573c697a79.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186115105 1074 "To plot $y=\\log_7 {(x+4)},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\log_7 {x}.$\n\n![](images/97/975f58b320e2293dbfaf267a3acfe9187438ae40add3603bac98d142b35c44c1.png)\n\n- Translate it by $4$ units to the left to get the graph of $y=\\log_7 {(x+4)}.$\n\n![](images/ce/ce5db8eb1adb7e84337427c1c769eabec9cb420b51a7e867f47e8d573c697a79.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115106 1071 "The diagram below shows the graph of $y=k \\log_5 (bx)$. Find the value of $b\\cdot k$.\n\n![](images/36/3698fc49edb443517ed0ee2cfa81c586c428f402df7028a8ec4b47702d8a4fad.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186115106 1071 "The diagram below shows the graph of $y=k \\log_5 (bx).$ Find the value of $b\\cdot k.$\n\n![](images/36/3698fc49edb443517ed0ee2cfa81c586c428f402df7028a8ec4b47702d8a4fad.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115106 1074 "Clearly,\n\n$$\nb \\neq 0\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac1b\n$$\n\ninto the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= k\\log_{5} (b \\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\\n&= k\\log_{5} 1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac1b, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nlies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(2,0)$ lies on the curve. Hence,\n\n$$\n(2,0) = \\left(\\dfrac1b,0\\right)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(10,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk\\log_{5} (bx) &= y \\\\\nk\\log_{5} (\\frac{1}{2}x) &= y \\\\\nk\\log_{5} (\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10) &= 3 \\\\\nk\\log_{5} 5 &= 3 \\\\\nk &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb\\cdot k = \\dfrac12 \\cdot 3 = \\dfrac32\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186115106 1074 "Clearly, $b \\neq 0.$ Substituting $x=\\dfrac1b$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{log}_{5}⁡(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =k{log}_{5}⁡1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $\\left(\\dfrac1b, 0\\right)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(2,0)$ lies on the curve. Hence, \n\n$$\n(2,0)  = \\left(\\dfrac1b,0\\right)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b=\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(10,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{log}_{5}⁡(bx) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}x) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10) & =3 \\\\ k{log}_{5}⁡5 & =3 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb\\cdot k = \\dfrac12 \\cdot 3 = \\dfrac32.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115147 1071 "Plot the graph of ![](images/54/54999da9704e7c5f8b341e30bb7af0d8c5c89b4cd91fa8be96d71521c30a6b95.svg)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115147 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4\\log_3 (x-2)+1$?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186115147 1074 "To plot\n\n$$\ny=4\\log_3 (x-2)+1\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\log_3 x$.![](images/45/452610c472df847bb