5396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563679 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563709 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563743 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563682 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563737 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563757 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563758 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115461 1050 780.0 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115484 1050 780.0 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115485 1050 840.0 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115674 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y = 5\\ln x$?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186115674 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115674 1074 "To plot $y=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/9a/9aa292639642f664a81078a5cf916a7f7efef4f4b4a944a85c17bfc63b11beb8.png)\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7c2d23d12059c5fe56ce44757825e6c95517e842caeded02b734bbfdc5f7945.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115757 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115757 1071 "Find $lim_(t → 1)[5f(t) + 2g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$f(t)|= ⟨t^{2}, t^{3}, \\frac{1}{t^{2}}⟩; g(t)|= ⟨5 - t^{3}, t^{2} - t + 2, 4⟩$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186115757 1071 "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 1}[5f(t)+2g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{3},\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨5-{t}^{3},{t}^{2}-t+2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115757 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 1}({t}^{2}),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 1}({t}^{3}),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 1}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{1}^{2},{1}^{3},\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 1}(5-{t}^{3}),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 1}({t}^{2}-t+2),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 1}(4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5-{1}^{3},{1}^{2}-1+2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[5f(t)+2g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}5f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 1}2g(t) \\\\  & =5{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =5⟨1,1,1⟩+2⟨4,2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,5,5⟩+⟨8,4,8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨13,9,13⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115759 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115759 1071 "Find the limit $lim_(t → π)[f(t) \\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n$f(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sin t \\\\ 1 + \\cos t \\\\ \\frac{4t}{π}\\end{bmatrix}, g(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ 2 + \\tan t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186115759 1071 "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to \\pi }[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{4t}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115759 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\pi }f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\pi }\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\pi }\\,(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\pi }\\,(\\frac{4t}{\\pi })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  \\\\ 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{\\pi }\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\pi }g(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\pi }\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\pi }\\,(2+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\pi }\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\ (2+\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\pi ) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\pi }[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to \\pi }f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to \\pi }g(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115761 1071 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous at $t = 0$?\n$f(t)|= \\cot ti + 3t^{3}j + (2t + 1)k; g(t)|= \\frac{2}{t}i + t^{3}j + \\sin tk; h(t)|= 2ti + \\cos tj + t^{4}k$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186115761 1071 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous at $t=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j+(2t+1)\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ h(t) & =2t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{t}^{4}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115761 1074 "A vector-valued function is continuous at a point if and only if each of its components is continuous at that point.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- Function $f(t)$ is **not** continuous at $t=0$ since its first component $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t$ is not continuous at $t=0.$ Indeed, the left and right limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to +0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=+\\infty  \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to -0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t$ doesn’t exist.\n\n- Function $g(t)$ is **not** continuous at $t=0$ since its first component $\\frac{2}{t}$ is not continuous at $t=0.$\n\n- Function $h(t)$ is continuous at $t=0$ because each of its components $2t$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and ${t}^{4}$ are continuous at $t=0.$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ $h(t)$ only." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115771 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115771 1071 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t ∈ (0, 1)$?\n$p(t)|= ⟨2t - 1, t^{2} + 4,-e^{t}⟩; q(t)|= ⟨(t + 1)^{4}, \\sqrt[3]{t}, \\ln t⟩; r(t)|= ⟨3t + 2, \\arctan t, t^{2} - 2⟩$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186115771 1071 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in (0,1)?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(t) & =⟨2t-1,\\,{t}^{2}+4,\\,-{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ q(t) & =⟨(t+1)^{4},\\,\\sqrt[3]{t},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩ \\\\ r(t) & =⟨3t+2,\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115771 1074 "All of these vector-valued functions are defined on $t\\in (0,1).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $t\\in (0,1)$ if and only if each component is differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $p(t)$ to find ${p}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,-{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${p}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we conclude that $p(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $q(t)$ to find ${q}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1)^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt[3]{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4(t+1)^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${q}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we have that $q(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $r(t)$ to find ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}},\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${r}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we conclude that $r(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ All the functions are differentiable on $(0,1).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115774 1071 "Find $lim_(t → 0)[3f(t) - g(t)]$ given that\n$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$f(t)|= ⟨\\cos t, t^{2} - 2, \\frac{t - 3}{t^{2} + 1}⟩; g(t)|= ⟨\\sin t, \\sqrt{t} + \\sqrt{2}, 2⟩$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186115774 1071 "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[3f(t)-g(t)]$ given that\n$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,{t}^{2}-2,\\frac{t-3}{{t}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\sqrt{t}+\\sqrt{2},2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115774 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}-2),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(\\frac{t-3}{{t}^{2}+1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,{0}^{2}-2,\\frac{0-3}{{0}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,-3⟩ \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(\\sqrt{t}+\\sqrt{2}),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\sqrt{0}+\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\sqrt{2},2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[3f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}3f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =3⟨1,-2,-3⟩-⟨0,\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-6,-9⟩-⟨0,\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3-0,-6-\\sqrt{2},-9-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-6-\\sqrt{2},-11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115804 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115804 1071 "Find $g(2)$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(2) = ⟨1, 1, 2⟩$ and\n$lim_(t → 2)[f(t) + 2g(t)] = ⟨ - 1, 7, 0⟩$." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186115804 1071 "Find $g(2)$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(2)=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)]=⟨-1,7,0⟩\\,.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115804 1074 "Using the constant multiple, and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 2}2g(t) \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =f(2)+2g(2) \\\\  & ={{⟨1,1,2⟩}_{⏟}}_{f(2)}+2g(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)]=⟨-1,7,0⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $g(2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,2⟩+2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩-⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =⟨-1,3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115805 1071 "Find the interval where the function $f(t) \\cdot g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n$f(t)|= \\mid t - 1 \\mid i + \\cos tj + \\sqrt{1 - t}k; g(t)|= t^{2}i + \\sqrt{t^{2} - 9}j + \\ln (3 - t)k$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186115805 1071 "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =|t-1|\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{1-t}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & ={t}^{2}\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9}\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186115805 1074 "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $|t-1|$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=1.$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\sqrt{1-t}$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,1)$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=1$ and the function is not defined for $t>1.$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,1).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component ${t}^{2}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The second component $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9}$ is differentiable for $(-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)$ is differentiable only on $(-\\infty ,3).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,-3).$ \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is $(-\\infty ,1)\\cap (-\\infty ,-3)=(-\\infty ,-3).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186116437 1064 "To find the limit of a vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually. So, if $\\mathbf f (t) = x(t) \\ \\mathbf {i} + y(t) \\ \\mathbf {j} +z(t) \\ \\mathbf {k}$ is our vector function, then the limit is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to a} \\mathbf f (t) = \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a} x(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {i} + \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}y(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {j} +\\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}z(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {k}\n$$\n\nThe limit on the left exists *if and only if* all the limits on the right exist too.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the vector function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\left( 5+t \\right) \\ \\mathbf {i} +  (t^{2}-1) \\ \\mathbf {j} + \\sin{t} \\ \\mathbf {k},\n$$\n\nand we want to compute the limit $\\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0} \\mathbf f (t).$ To do this, we find the limit of each component function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}(5+t)}_{⏟}}_{=\\,5}\\,]i+[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}-1)\\,}_{⏟}}_{=\\,-1}]j+[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,}_{⏟}}_{=\\,0}]k \\\\  & =5\\text{ }i+(-1)\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186116437 1064 "To find the limit of a vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually. So, if $\\mathbf f (t) = x(t) \\ \\mathbf {i} + y(t) \\ \\mathbf {j} +z(t) \\ \\mathbf {k}$ is our vector function, then the limit is computed as follows: $$\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to a} \\mathbf f (t) = \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a} x(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {i} + \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}y(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {j} +\\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}z(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {k}$$ The limit on the left exists *if and only if* all the limits on the right exist too.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the vector function $$\\mathbf f (t) = \\left( 5+t \\right) \\ \\mathbf {i} + (t^{2}-1) \\ \\mathbf {j} + \\sin{t} \\ \\mathbf {k},$$ and we want to compute the limit $\\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0} \\mathbf f (t).$ To do this, we find the limit of each component function as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}(5+t)}_{⏟}}_{=\\,5}\\,]i+[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}-1)\\,}_{⏟}}_{=\\,-1}]j+[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}t\\,}_{⏟}}_{=\\,0}]k \\\\ & =5\\text{ }i+(-1)\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k \\\\ & =⟨5,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186116438 1064 "Many properties of limits of scalar functions extend naturally to limits of vector functions. For instance, if $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are vector functions and $p,q$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t \\to a}\\left[p \\ \\mathbf f(t) + q \\ \\mathbf g(t)\\right] =  p \\ \\lim_{t \\to a}\\mathbf f(t) + q \\ \\lim_{t \\to a}\\mathbf g(t).\n$$\n\nWe also have the following property for the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to a}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to a}f(t)‖,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\left\\| \\mathbf f(t) \\right\\|$ denotes the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf f(t)$." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186116438 1064 "Many properties of limits of scalar functions extend naturally to limits of vector functions. For instance, if $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are vector functions and $p,q$ are constants, then\n\n$$\\lim_{t \\to a}\\left[p \\ \\mathbf f(t) + q \\ \\mathbf g(t)\\right] = p \\ \\lim_{t \\to a}\\mathbf f(t) + q \\ \\lim_{t \\to a}\\mathbf g(t).$$\n\nWe also have the following property for the magnitude:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to a}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to a}f(t)‖,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $\\left\\| \\mathbf f(t) \\right\\|$ denotes the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf f(t)$." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186116439 1064 "For two vector functions, the limit of a product is equal to the product of the limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to a}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to a}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to a}g(t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to a}[f(t)\\times g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to a}f(t)\\times {lim}_{t\\to a}g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $\\mathbf f(t)\\cdot \\mathbf g(t)$ and $\\mathbf f(t) \\times \\mathbf g(t)$ are the dot product and the cross product of $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t),$ respectively." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186116439 1064 "For two vector functions, the limit of a product is equal to the product of the limits:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to a}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to a}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to a}g(t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to a}[f(t)\\times g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to a}f(t)\\times {lim}_{t\\to a}g(t)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nHere, $\\mathbf f(t)\\cdot \\mathbf g(t)$ and $\\mathbf f(t) \\times \\mathbf g(t)$ are the dot product and the cross product of $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t),$ respectively." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186116443 1064 "Determining the continuity of a vector function is very similar to determining the continuity of a scalar function.  A vector function $\\mathbf f (t)$ is **continuous** at a point $t_0$ (in its domain) if\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\rightarrow t_0} \\mathbf f (t) = \\mathbf f (t_0).\n$$\n\nFor example, the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t) = \\left \\langle e^t,  \\dfrac{1}{t} \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nis continuous at $t=1$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 1}({e}^{t}),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 1}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{1},\\,\\frac{1}{1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨e,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit of a vector function is determined by the limits of the components, a vector function is continuous at a point $t_0$ *if and only if* each of its components is continuous at that point.\n\nWe say that a vector function is **continuous on the interval** $t\\in[a,b]$ if it is continuous at every point of this interval." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186116443 1064 "Determining the continuity of a vector function is very similar to determining the continuity of a scalar function. A vector function $\\mathbf f (t)$ is **continuous** at a point $t_0$ (in its domain) if\n\n$$\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\rightarrow t_0} \\mathbf f (t) = \\mathbf f (t_0).$$\n\nFor example, the vector function\n\n$$\\mathbf f(t) = \\left \\langle e^t, \\dfrac{1}{t} \\right \\rangle$$\n\nis continuous at $t=1$ because\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 1}({e}^{t}),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 1}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\ & =⟨{e}^{1},\\,\\frac{1}{1}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨e,\\,1⟩ \\\\ & =f(1)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince the limit of a vector function is determined by the limits of the components, a vector function is continuous at a point $t_0$ *if and only if* each of its components is continuous at that point.\n\nWe say that a vector function is **continuous on the interval** $t\\in[a,b]$ if it is continuous at every point of this interval." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186116444 1064 "Similar to scalar-valued functions, the vector function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is **differentiable** at a point $t = t_0$ in the domain of the function if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\mathbf{f}(t_0+h) - \\mathbf{f}(t_0)}{h}\n$$\n\nThe value of the limit is then called the **derivative** of $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ at $t = t_0$ and is denoted as $\\mathbf{f}'(t_0)$. In general, a vector function is differentiable *if and only if* all of its components are differentiable.\n\nFor example, is the following vector function $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ differentiable at $t=0$?\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{g}(t) = \\big\\langle t, \\: |\\,t\\,|, \\: \\sin t \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nNotice that the second component, $|\\,t\\,|,$ is not differentiable at $t=0.$ Since one of the component functions is not differentiable at $t=0,$ we conclude that the overall vector function $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ is not differentiable at $t=0.$ \n\nWe say that a vector function is **differentiable on an open interval** $t \\in (a,b)$ if it is differentiable at *every* point $t \\in (a,b).$ This means that each component of the function must be differentiable at every point of $(a,b).$\n\nOur function $\\mathbf{g}(t)$, mentioned above, is differentiable on $(-\\infty, 0) \\cup (0, \\infty)$ since all of its components are differentiable there.\n\nFinally, a function is said to be **continuously differentiable** if it is differentiable and its derivative is continuous." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186116444 1064 "Similar to scalar-valued functions, the vector function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is **differentiable** at a point $t = t_0$ in the domain of the function if the following limit exists: $$\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\mathbf{f}(t_0+h) - \\mathbf{f}(t_0)}{h}$$ The value of the limit is then called the **derivative** of $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ at $t = t_0$ and is denoted as $\\mathbf{f}'(t_0)$. In general, a vector function is differentiable *if and only if* all of its components are differentiable.\n\nFor example, is the following vector function $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ differentiable at $t=0$? $$\\mathbf{g}(t) = \\big\\langle t, \\: |\\,t\\,|, \\: \\sin t \\big\\rangle$$\n\nNotice that the second component, $|\\,t\\,|,$ is not differentiable at $t=0.$ Since one of the component functions is not differentiable at $t=0,$ we conclude that the overall vector function $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ is not differentiable at $t=0.$\n\nWe say that a vector function is **differentiable on an open interval** $t \\in (a,b)$ if it is differentiable at *every* point $t \\in (a,b).$ This means that each component of the function must be differentiable at every point of $(a,b).$\n\nOur function $\\mathbf{g}(t)$, mentioned above, is differentiable on $(-\\infty, 0) \\cup (0, \\infty)$ since all of its components are differentiable there.\n\nFinally, a function is said to be **continuously differentiable** if it is differentiable and its derivative is continuous." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186116516 1064 "Suppose we are given the function\n\n$$\ny=5\\log_6(x-2)\n$$\n\nHow do we plot its graph?\n\nAs usual, we can do this by combining transformations of the curve $y=\\log_6 x$.\n\n- We start with the graph of $y=\\log_6 x{:}$![](images/a8/a85eb96e169e43568da76e33ee7f997af9196d26e867d7e7db1b2ddea0725d2a.png)\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=\\log_6{(x-2)}$.![](images/24/248a60c13ac422aeace5ba19f9f25716b188c19dbe04f5970287c587adb44c85.png)\nNotice that points $(1,0)$ and $(6,1)$ on the graph of $y=\\log_6 x$ map to the points $(3,0)$ and $(8,1)$ on the graph of $y=\\log_6 (x-2)$, respectively.\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=5\\log_6{(x-2)}$.![](images/a6/a67b409068f67d81f0217aaeb30f76bd63c6501376d2adc567db7c4f319d7144.png)\n\nSo, to plot the curve of a transformed logarithmic function, we apply subsequent transformations presented in the given function, as usual." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186116516 1064 "Suppose we are given the function\n\n$$\ny=5\\log_6(x-2).\n$$\n\nHow do we plot its graph?\n\nAs usual, we can do this by combining transformations of the curve $y=\\log_6 x.$\n\n- We start with the graph of $y=\\log_6 x{:}$\n\n![](images/a8/a85eb96e169e43568da76e33ee7f997af9196d26e867d7e7db1b2ddea0725d2a.png)\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=\\log_6{(x-2)}.$\n\n![](images/24/248a60c13ac422aeace5ba19f9f25716b188c19dbe04f5970287c587adb44c85.png)\n\nNotice that points $(1,0)$ and $(6,1)$ on the graph of $y=\\log_6 x$ map to the points $(3,0)$ and $(8,1)$ on the graph of $y=\\log_6 (x-2),$ respectively.\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=5\\log_6{(x-2)}.$\n\n![](images/a6/a67b409068f67d81f0217aaeb30f76bd63c6501376d2adc567db7c4f319d7144.png)\n\nSo, to plot the curve of a transformed logarithmic function, we apply subsequent transformations presented in the given function, as usual." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186117963 1078 #edb/ref 17592186117964 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186119808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563719 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186119808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563732 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186119808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563749 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186119856 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563678 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186120304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186120304 1071 "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π: r = sb + tc$, $t, s ∈ R$, where $b = ⟨1, 5, 8⟩$, $c = ⟨ - 1,-1,-3⟩$, and $u = ⟨ - 3, 5,-7⟩$. In the affirmative case, find the value of $s \\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u = sb + tc$." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186120304 1071 "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc,$ $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨1,5,8⟩$, $c=⟨-1,-1,-3⟩$, and  $u=⟨-3,5,-7⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186120304 1074 "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s-t=-3 \\\\ 5s-t=5 \\\\ 8s-3t=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & -3 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 20 \\\\ 8 & -3 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-8{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=-8,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $s$ and $t$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc=u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $u$ does *not* lie on the plane $Π.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186121035 1064 "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n![](images/55/55c749dccc54c252b9120f148e383f395c09fb4cd4bec0e1cce8ed441ecb6199.svg)\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n![](images/21/2181b471f34906eeaea7889abbba37c797a2f9624cee455b43b00fc048247cd1.svg)\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186121035 1064 "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n![](images/55/55c749dccc54c252b9120f148e383f395c09fb4cd4bec0e1cce8ed441ecb6199.svg)\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n![](images/21/2181b471f34906eeaea7889abbba37c797a2f9624cee455b43b00fc048247cd1.svg)\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186124437 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563687 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186124438 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563730 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563656 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186124458 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563741 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186124458 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563750 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186124458 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563760 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186124459 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563713 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186124459 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563763 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186124460 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563650 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186124460 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563689 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186133748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563761 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186166806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563720 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186166806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563744 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186166808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563668 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186166808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563699 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186166809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563651 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186179320 1078 #edb/ref 17592186179321 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186179680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563665 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186179681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563756 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186179751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563736 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186179764 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563718 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186179764 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563723 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186180296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186180296 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation $2(x + 4)^{3} = 686$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186180296 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation $2(x+4)^{3}=686.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186180296 1074 "We can solve this equation by isolating the $(x+4)^{3}$ term and then taking the $3$rd root of each side.\n\nFirst, let's isolate the $(x+4)^{3}$ term on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+4)^{3} & =686 \\\\ (x+4)^{3} & =343\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the $3$rd root of each side and solve for $x.$ Since $n=3$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{3} & =343 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(x+4)^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{343} \\\\ x+4 & =\\sqrt[3]{{7}^{3}} \\\\ x+4 & =7 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186184320 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563692 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186184320 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563731 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186184321 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563675 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186191116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191117 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186191116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191118 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186191116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191119 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186191116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191120 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186191116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191121 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186191116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186562944 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186191116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563045 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186191116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563092 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186191116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563218 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186191116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563449 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186191116 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563218 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186191877 1078 #edb/ref 17592186191878 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186192177 1078 #edb/ref 17592186192179 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186192764 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563700 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186192764 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563734 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186192802 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563671 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186192802 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563753 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186193178 1071 "![](images/c2/c2452160f8af4651bdc78756b7c0df8402de185ba7a7bedbc1c1a676b342af97.png)\n\nConsider the set $A,$ shown above. The boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186193178 1071 "![](images/c2/c2452160f8af4651bdc78756b7c0df8402de185ba7a7bedbc1c1a676b342af97.png)\n\nConsider the set $A,$ shown above. The boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186193178 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/eea68ab4e4b440609fcc4f6c2d40c4dc9fc394fc45f1047ec9c658729322749a.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the parabola ${y}^{2}=4x.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{y}^{2}=4x\\,\\}.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186193178 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/eea68ab4e4b440609fcc4f6c2d40c4dc9fc394fc45f1047ec9c658729322749a.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the parabola ${y}^{2}=4x.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{y}^{2}=4x\\,}.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186193205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186193205 1071 "Consider the set $A$, defined by\n$A = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 4x^{2} + y^{2} > 4}$.\n\nThe boundary of $A$ is given by\n$∂A = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: A A A A A}$.\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186193205 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4\\}.\n$$\n\nThe boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186193205 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b0/b0c3e30fcb7e748a80cdf98892291599967151343efe671aca790b2528039c4c.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4\\,\\}.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186193324 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y<0\\}.\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<2,\\,\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186193324 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y<0\\}.\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<2,\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186193325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186193325 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n1. $[1, 2]∪[3, 4]$\n2. $[0, 1)$\n3. $[0, 1)∪(1, 2]$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186193325 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\nI. $[1,2]\\cup [3,4]$\n\nII. $[0,1)$\n\nIII. $[0,1)\\cup (1,2]$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186193325 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $[1,2]\\cup [3,4]$ is closed. It consists of two parts, where the boundary points are $1,2,3,$ and $4.$ All of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other sets are not closed because:\n\n- $[0,1)$ has a boundary point $1$, which does not lie in the set.\n\n- $[0,1)\\cup (1,2]$ has a boundary point $1$, which does not lie in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186193375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186193375 1071 "Which of the following sets are open?\n\n1. $(- ∞, 1]$\n2. $(0, 1)∪(5, 6)$\n3. $[- 3,-1)∪(0, 1]$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186193375 1071 "Which of the following sets are open?\n\nI. $(-\\infty ,1]$\n\nII. $(0,1)\\cup (5,6)$\n\nIII. $[-3,-1)\\cup (0,1]$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186193375 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $(0,1)\\cup (5,6)$ is open. It consists of two parts, where the boundary points are $0$, $1$, $5$, and $6.$ None of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other sets are not open because:\n\n- $(-\\infty ,1]$ has a boundary point $1$ and it lies in the set.\n\n- $[-3,-1)\\cup (0,1]$ has four boundary points $-3$, $-1$, $0$, and $1$, and the points $-3$ and $1$ lie in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186204009 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563655 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186204011 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563704 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186229142 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563755 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186229144 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563702 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186233518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563553 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186233518 1071 "Which of the following is a horizontal asymptote of $y={log}_{6}x?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186233518 1071 "Which of the following is a horizontal asymptote of $y={log}_{6}⁡x?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186233518 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{6}x.$\n\n![](images/41/4177d9520b3758b29926eba7a7a74ed4c508d861e3c343f46c372ec79500fc9d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note that for $x>1$, the graph grows very slowly as we increase $x.$ \n\nWe say that $y\\to \\infty $ (slowly) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, there are no horizontal asymptotes." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186233518 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{6}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/41/4177d9520b3758b29926eba7a7a74ed4c508d861e3c343f46c372ec79500fc9d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note that for $x>1$, the graph grows very slowly as we increase $x.$ \n\nWe say that $y\\to \\infty $ (slowly) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, there are no horizontal asymptotes." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186233519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563614 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186233519 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y={log}_{6}x?$ \n\nI. - It has no zeros\nII. - $y\\to 6$ as $x\\to \\infty $\nIII. - It has a vertical asymptote at $x=0$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186233519 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y={log}_{6}⁡x?$ \n\nI. It has no zeros\n\nII. $y\\to 6$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n\nIII. It has a vertical asymptote at $x=0$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186233519 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y={log}_{6}x.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8d3f9dc995d8801d451b15129860362efa772178cdc3ef07be5adb6a1da5c45.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has a zero at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As $x$ increases, $y$ increases without bound. Therefore, $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186233519 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y={log}_{6}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8d3f9dc995d8801d451b15129860362efa772178cdc3ef07be5adb6a1da5c45.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has a zero at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As $x$ increases, $y$ increases without bound. Therefore, $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186233625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563555 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186234082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563578 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186234082 1071 "What's the range of $f(x)={log}_{9}x?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 false] [17592186234082 1071 "What's the range of $f(x)={log}_{9}⁡x?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186234082 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{9}x.$\n\n![](images/40/40664f83ba162a93ed06970798d37798db9