e] [17592186563642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563133 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563388 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563642 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563642 1077 "selection" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563642 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563388 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563642 1079 #uuid "ff3c854a-2ec4-5aac-9265-718757aee35e" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186562960 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563023 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563256 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563286 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563375 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563643 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563643 1077 "selection" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563643 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563375 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563643 1079 #uuid "ffb11692-b94a-52a8-a712-f1575499549c" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563644 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048652 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563644 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048826 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563644 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049074 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563644 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049399 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563644 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050396 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563644 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050622 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563644 1088 #edb/ref 17592186051017 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563644 1089 #edb/ref 17592186563645 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563644 1090 #uuid "e8d03789-dde0-513c-a07d-47ec47074087" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563645 1084 #edb/ref 17592186563771 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563645 1084 #edb/ref 17592186563780 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563645 1084 #edb/ref 17592186563783 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563645 1084 #edb/ref 17592186563784 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563645 1084 #edb/ref 17592186563808 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563645 1084 #edb/ref 17592186563810 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563645 1084 #edb/ref 17592186563815 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563645 1085 "The sales manager of a company that produces wooden furniture analyses the weekly profit made by creating $x$ wooden tables per week. They found that the weekly profit $p(x),$ in dollars, can be modeled by the quadratic function\n\n$$\np(x)=a{x}^{2}+bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563645 1086 #uuid "bb1bcb11-dd3e-5ef2-acf5-2bd2feb4ce5d" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563645 1087 #edb/ref 17592186563808 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563624 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563646 1069 "q-84911" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563646 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563646 1071 "The axis of symmetry of $y=\\frac{1}{3}(k-x)(5x+3k)$ is $x=\\frac{1}{10}.$ The value of $k=$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563646 1072 #uuid "04b365f6-2d57-5574-aec1-e0a7c1397133" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563646 1074 "First, we calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{10},$ we will set our result equal to $\\frac{1}{10}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=\\frac{1}{3}(k-x)(5x+3k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=k$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{3k}{5}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k-\\frac{3k}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5k}{5}-\\frac{3k}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2k}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{5} & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563647 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563588 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563647 1069 "q-127820" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563647 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563647 1071 "Find $f(5)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(5)=⟨-1,1,-2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩\\,.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563647 1072 #uuid "05950ac2-f758-5682-b193-f12241b0a085" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563647 1074 "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 5}[3g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to 5}g(t) \\\\  & =f(5)+3g(5) \\\\  & =f(5)+3{{⟨-1,1,-2⟩}_{⏟}}_{g(5)} \\\\  & =f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(5):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩ & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨1,3,1⟩-⟨-3,3,-6⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨4,0,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563536 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563648 1069 "q-127810" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563648 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563648 1071 "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ \\sqrt{t} \\\\ t+3\\end{aligned}]$  is continuous." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563648 1072 #uuid "0b1d012f-068b-5ef8-8288-effe2d51246e" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563648 1074 "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one by one.\n\n- The function $\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}$ is continuous everywhere except the excluded points $t=\\pm 1.$\n\n- The function $\\sqrt{t}$ is continuous for $t\\ge 0.$\n\n- The function $t+3$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous for all $t\\ge 0$ except the point $t=1.$ So the interval of continuity is $[0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563649 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563639 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563649 1069 "q-78269" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563649 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563649 1071 "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{t},\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{2t}-1}{t},\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563649 1072 #uuid "0e9c7e83-813e-5902-881b-22d16eab32e5" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563649 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,2{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{(2t)},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0+2}{0+1},\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the second component of ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)$ we used a special limit ${lim}_{u\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡u}{u}=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2t}-1}{t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{({e}^{2t}-1)^{′}}{(t)^{′}},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{1},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{0},\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the first component of ${lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t)$ we used L'Hopital's Rule.\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563575 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563650 1069 "q-130802" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563650 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563650 1071 "Consider the function $g(x,y)={y}^{x}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,e)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨4,-3⟩?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563650 1072 #uuid "0fb365d6-3918-560a-af36-ae398310b595" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563650 1074 "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,e)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,e).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{x}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{y}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,x{y}^{x-1}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,e) & =⟨{e}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e,1{e}^{1-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨e,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,e)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,e) & =∇g(1,e)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨e,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨4,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{e(4)+1(-3)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4e-3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563564 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563651 1069 "q-97910" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563651 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{16-(3x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563651 1072 #uuid "1369cbfd-a46c-5064-baa1-cbe5720fb540" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563651 1074 "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{16-(3x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{16-16(\\frac{1}{16}(3x+2)^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{16}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{4}(3x+2))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{1}{4}(3x+2).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{4}(3x+2))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{16}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{3x+2}{4})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{3x+2}{4}|>1.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563638 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563652 1069 "q-70565" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563652 1071 "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(-7)=⟨6,0,-2⟩$ and $g(-7)=⟨2,-1,0⟩,$ determine\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)].\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563652 1072 #uuid "1380f470-1cae-5a0e-b171-8e1423867447" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563652 1074 "Using the constant multiple and the addition rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to -7}\\frac{f(t)}{2}+{lim}_{t\\to -7}3g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to -7}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to -7}g(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}f(-7)+3g(-7) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨6,0,-2⟩+3⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-1⟩+⟨6,-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9,-3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563572 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563653 1069 "q-106169" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563653 1071 "The axis of symmetry of $y=(x-2k)(x+3k)$ is $x=-2.$ The value of $k=$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563653 1072 #uuid "143580a6-1bb7-5c0f-bc9f-072a4a4b0e12" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563653 1074 "First, we calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-2,$ we will set our result equal to $-2.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=(x-2k)(x+3k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=2k$ and ${x}_{2}=-3k.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k+(-3k)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-k}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-2.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-k}{2} & =-2 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563599 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563654 1069 "q-311955" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563654 1071 "| **$x$** | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$f(x)$** | $0$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nThe graph of a parabola $y=f(x)$ passes through the points given by their $(x,y)$-coordinates in the table above. The parabola opens downward. What is the $x$-coordinate of the parabola's vertex?\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563654 1072 #uuid "14e0eb8a-dac7-5f73-9945-6c6e124a2b27" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563654 1074 "Notice that the roots of the parabola are given. They are \n\n$$\nx=2\\,\\text{and}\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nIf the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k),$ then by the average of the roots formula, $h$ is simply the average of the two roots. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{2+8}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563537 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563655 1069 "q-251850" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563655 1071 "Consider two sets of $20$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{20},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{20}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=60,\\,{S}_{yy}=116,\\,{S}_{xy}=100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, ${\\sigma }_{y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563655 1072 #uuid "17851fea-30ed-54e9-89be-985c48bef200" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563655 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the variance of ${y}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{y}^{2} & =\\frac{{S}_{yy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{yy}$ is the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${y}_{i}.$\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{yy}=116.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{y}^{2} & =\\frac{116}{20} \\\\  & =5.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563558 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563656 1069 "q-132608" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563656 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=0.$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(y+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563656 1072 #uuid "19ce6755-ce5c-5344-86b9-dca6271615dd" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563656 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0) & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2(\\frac{1}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $x-z+\\frac{1}{2}=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-k)=i+k$ is also parallel to it." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563527 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563657 1069 "q-167914" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563657 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4{log}_{3}⁡(x-2)-1?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563657 1072 #uuid "1a4b2537-96ba-591d-822a-13598207943c" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563657 1074 "To plot $y=4{log}_{3}⁡(x-2)-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y={log}_{3}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/76/76bd0cabc680c0f0ac7d21c2b1122bff3e05bd0b544a2043e9f8a6aa6056db06.png)\n\n- Translate it by $2$ unit to the right to get the graph of $y={log}_{3}⁡(x-2).$\n\n![](images/c3/c385f9b1786078fab7ee97b5b266454be8afe32dd3f82bfe2c35bc1ea1ff424d.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=4{log}_{3}⁡(x-2).$\n\n![](images/a7/a7a1802a657832efcd1fd2ca8d2c34b9226b1b5324636f9779bda68b4f8646d1.png)\n\n- Finally, translate the result by $1$ unit down to get the graph of $y=4{log}_{3}⁡(x-2)-1.$\n\n![](images/34/3483850384b487ec9bb497cb328cf642fccf5605f9a766492d59082f3d0d5d12.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563540 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563658 1069 "q-70566" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563658 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563658 1071 "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{t-4}\\,j-5{t}^{2}\\,k$ is differentiable." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563658 1072 #uuid "22fc3cb9-9f8c-550e-b1a7-090b46545ee2" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563658 1074 "The vector-valued function $f(t)$ is defined on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$ Let's study the differentiability of its components on this interval one-by-one.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\sqrt{t}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{t})}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is not defined at $t=0,$ we conclude that  $\\sqrt{t}$ is not differentiable at this point.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\frac{1}{t-4}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{t-4})}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{(t-4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $\\frac{1}{t-4}$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  $-5{t}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{(-5{t}^{2})}^{′}=-10t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $-5{t}^{2}$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTaking into account that all the components of $f(t)$ are differentiable for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ except the point $t=0,$ we conclude that $f(t)$  is differentiable on $(0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563616 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563659 1069 "q-311952" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563659 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563659 1071 "| **$x$** | $-4$ | $-1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$f(x)$** | $0$ | $-12$ | $0$ |\n\nThe graph of a parabola $y=f(x)$ passes through the points given by their $(x,y)$-coordinates in the table above. What is the $x$-coordinate of the parabola's vertex?\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563659 1072 #uuid "29e68a84-4d10-52b0-a908-447f3d997c55" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563659 1074 "Notice that the roots of the parabola are given. They are \n\n$$\nx=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nIf the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k),$ then by the average of the roots formula, $h$ is simply the average of the two roots. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{-4+3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563550 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563660 1069 "q-338390" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563660 1071 "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{t\\to -1}(\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563660 1072 #uuid "2b9acb7e-8235-5df0-907d-863119079541" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563660 1074 "Applying the sum and root rules, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to -1}(\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}}) & ={lim}_{t\\to -1}\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-{lim}_{t\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{{({lim}_{t\\to -1}(2t+1))}^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{{({lim}_{t\\to -1}({t}^{2}+t+1))}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(2(-1)+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{(((-1)^{2}+(-1)+1))^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(-2+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{((1-1+1))^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(-1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{(1)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{-1}-\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563594 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563661 1069 "q-127734" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563661 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563661 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t),$ if $f(t)=⟨{e}^{t-2},\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,\\frac{3}{t+2}⟩.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563661 1072 #uuid "2c409cd1-fe1f-5e01-a403-dbc5d06f9573" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563661 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)=⟨{lim}_{t\\to 2}{e}^{t-2},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{3}{t+2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}{e}^{t-2} & ={e}^{2-2} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-t} & =\\sqrt{6-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{3}{t+2} & =\\frac{3}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)=⟨1,2,\\frac{3}{4}⟩.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563627 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563662 1069 "q-32979" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563662 1071 "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 1}‖f(t)‖$, if $f(t)=\\frac{4+t}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1}\\,j+\\sqrt{9t}\\,k.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563662 1072 #uuid "2cd66931-0513-5d1c-9823-4729248ee301" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563662 1074 "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{4+t}{5})\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1})\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\sqrt{9t})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{4+t}{5} & =\\frac{4+1}{5}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{(t-1)(t+1)} \\\\  & =({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{1}{t+1})\\cdot ({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{t-1}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{1+1})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\sqrt{9t} & =\\sqrt{9(1)}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)=i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+3\\,k.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $1$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+3\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{41}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563663 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563580 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563663 1069 "q-130314" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563663 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563663 1071 "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t-2 \\\\ \\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563663 1072 #uuid "2e0611fa-e2b7-5168-b829-407939c6ea09" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563663 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}3{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{0} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+9}{0+3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t-2) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{2}^{t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0-2 \\\\ \\frac{3(0)-2}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{0}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+4{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563615 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563664 1069 "q-78268" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563664 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563664 1071 "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563664 1072 #uuid "33768c70-a4a8-56c5-b929-58df88c6ba1b" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563664 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1}) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{′}}{(t-1)^{′}}) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{1}{t}) \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the first component of ${lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)$ we used L'Hopital's Rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1+1}}{1+1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+0\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563592 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563665 1069 "q-91369" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563665 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563665 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation $(x-3)^{7}=-128.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563665 1072 #uuid "351787b8-999c-59c8-b44f-8fe6c60247fb" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563665 1074 "We can solve this equation by taking the $7$th root of each side and solving for $x.$\nSince $n=7$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{7} & =-128 \\\\ \\sqrt[7]{(x-3)^{7}} & =\\sqrt[7]{-128} \\\\ x-3 & =\\sqrt[7]{(-2)^{7}} \\\\ x-3 & =-2 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=1.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563666 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563530 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563666 1069 "q-167217" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563666 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563666 1071 "If the company produces fifteen wooden tables in a week, the weekly profit will be $\\$4\\,050.$ Find an expression for $b$ in terms of $a.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563666 1072 #uuid "354145f6-5e93-540e-9ff9-3f90c1a259ed" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563666 1074 "In part 1, we found that $p(15)=225a+15b.$ So, we need to solve the equation $225a+15b=4\\,050$ for the parameter $b.$\n\nWe need to isolate the variable $b.$ To do this, we treat $a$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we subtract $225a$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}225a+15b & =4\\,050 \\\\ 225a+15b-225a & =4\\,050-225a \\\\ 15b & =4\\,050-225a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $15:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15b & =4\\,050-225a \\\\ \\frac{15b}{15} & =\\frac{4\\,050-225a}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{15}b}{\\cancel{15}} & =\\frac{4\\,050}{15}-\\frac{225a}{15} \\\\ b & =270-15a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $b=270-15a.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563585 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563667 1069 "q-32987" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563667 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ (1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563667 1072 #uuid "37a31447-f0c5-5a53-afc7-a1edb4864b07" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563667 1074 "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} & =\\sqrt{0+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s)}^{′}}{{s}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)} & ={lim}_{u\\to 0}(1+u)^{1/u}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ e\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563522 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563668 1069 "q-97904" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563668 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563668 1071 "$\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{|x-5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+27}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563668 1072 #uuid "38a6fd7f-85a4-5b9d-9914-3bd5a2ab420c" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563668 1074 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+27 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2}+27 \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{|x-5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+27}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{|x-5|\\sqrt{2+(x-5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}}|\\sqrt{2+2{(\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}}|\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}}|\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x-5}{\\sqrt{2}})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563529 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563669 1069 "q-167216" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563669 1071 "In terms of $a$ and $b,\\,p(15)=\\,${{field-1}}." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563669 1072 #uuid "3979b7c5-84a5-5fc8-9e6c-343a28511a49" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563669 1074 "We substitute $x=15$ into the expression for $p(x)$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =a{x}^{2}+bx \\\\ p(15) & =a(15)^{2}+b(15) \\\\  & =225a+15b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(15)=225a+15b.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563625 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563670 1069 "q-113185" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563670 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}+9}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563670 1072 #uuid "3a9630e4-9e0f-5dfd-9e56-64f09a2aef77" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563670 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7{x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(7{x}^{2}-4)}{(2+\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(7{x}^{2}-4)}{(2+\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{7{x}^{2}-4}{2+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(7{x}^{2}-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563601 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563671 1069 "q-161586" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563671 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563671 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1\\}.\n$$\n\nThe boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563671 1072 #uuid "3c4b52d1-eec1-5fd6-92e3-909a0fe3f144" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563671 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c2b4e3d11f16920ce24a279289808d3f5dc4e68563525f1417c8767a5446b461.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\,\\}.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563544 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563672 1069 "q-7870" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563672 1071 "![](images/98/98b9df96585a5e7067f229ed85a2e60309fbc56865d9bdf61d98aed68ac70ac2.png)\n\nThe point $A$ with coordinates $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ and the point ${A}^{′}$ with coordinates $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax),$ shown above. What is the value of $a?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563672 1072 #uuid "3efa74bc-65a6-5cab-855c-4b517911be86" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563672 1074 "The graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax)$ can be obtained from $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ by a horizontal stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{a}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)$ that lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ becomes \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{a}\\cdot 4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)=(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax).$ \n\nTherefore, $a=4.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563547 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563673 1069 "q-6976" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563673 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{9-{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}+x}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563673 1072 #uuid "4144a704-615f-5e15-9570-1e799a640e02" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563673 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{9-{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}}-1)}{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}}-1)}{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-1}{2+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563607 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563674 1069 "q-98092" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563674 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563674 1072 #uuid "416b8a87-9deb-532d-86eb-39a4fa9daecb" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563674 1074 "To plot $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f12e7576bb07678189e775c8e2cc98f621e740b45c558c743423a707253c9f81.png)\n\n- Translate it by $3$ units to the left to get the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](images/e3/e313515347d6bebbaa76d047fd0900a5e712f64bed05c796907a54bf81628201.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](images/94/94ae95babe9a30030c18d67347b59999e3e25c201afe8aebf5ae4197c42138d6.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563590 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563675 1069 "q-141851" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563675 1071 "$\\sqrt[3]{4s}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{7{t}^{2}}=$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563675 1072 #uuid "41a2ad72-53df-54b1-8f6d-95041c4754db" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563675 1074 "We apply the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{4s}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{7{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{4s\\cdot 7{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{28s{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563582 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563676 1069 "q-338364" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563676 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563676 1071 "![](images/94/94027c25ef78ae46d0deb4b577339daf878efd71b71029a9575f9c8ed9096e2c.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the function $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563676 1072 #uuid "41ad1c03-af11-5517-b3f9-c73f27dc13ff" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563676 1074 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=3.$\n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3}={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2))}^{3} \\\\  & =(3-2)^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3} & =(1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563519 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563677 1069 "q-110654" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563677 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563677 1071 "![](images/58/58e4554f6e5081ec5db6876222a7eeea24135743e4b68e25204084e097066ea4.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be graphs of odd functions?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563677 1072 #uuid "422c65f7-5866-5757-b32d-736e922491f4" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563677 1074 "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graphs II, III, and IV do not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, it is **not** an odd function.\n\n- On the other hand, graph I does have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that graph  I  show odd functions.\n\n![](images/3b/3b5e234a3416a5e7100468b81f170171a873508eb0c87bba126009daca9e49bc.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563678 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563609 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563678 1069 "q-105235" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563678 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563678 1071 "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563678 1072 #uuid "435bef4b-b3af-5768-973e-b4aa50ac129d" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563678 1074 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -9 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 10 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 10 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-6.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-6\\,⇒\\,k=6.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563621 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563679 1069 "q-127855" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563679 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563679 1071 "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k$ is differentiable." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563679 1072 #uuid "43d07e17-8355-5232-ab17-51baae273661" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563679 1074 "The vector-valued function $f(t)$ is defined on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$ Let's study the differentiability of its components on this interval one by one.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)}^{′}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1})}^{′}=\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is not defined at $t=\\pm 1,$ we conclude that  $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}$ is not differentiable at this points.\n\n- The derivative of  $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is\n\n$$\n{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTaking into account that all the components of $f(t)$ are differentiable for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ except the points $t=\\pm 1,$ we conclude that $f(t)$  is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563565 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563680 1069 "q-122171" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563680 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563680 1071 "![](images/0b/0bff27a71fc8421d51e69a677260f60a1e421c09fe3969f70d4c07bf1ac58576.png)\n\nFind the tangent of $∠P.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563680 1072 #uuid "44ffb250-1ebe-5832-a06b-038dd7ff73b2" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563680 1074 "Recall the definition of tangent: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡P=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}=\\frac{QR}{PQ}=\\frac{QR}{3.5}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing length $QR.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =\\sqrt{P{R}^{2}-P{Q}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{12.5}^{2}-{3.5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{156.25-12.25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡P & =\\frac{QR}{3.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{120}{35} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563640 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563681 1069 "q-5870" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563681 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563681 1071 "The table below defines the function $f(x).$ Is the function even, odd, or neither?\n\n| **$x$** | $-6$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $85$ | $75$ | $30$ | $-30$ | $-75$ | $-85$ |" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563681 1072 #uuid "488cdf32-d755-59bf-889e-a9cbc58dc41a" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563681 1074 "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain of $f$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain of $f$\n\nFor our function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6) & =85=-f(6), \\\\ f(-4) & =75=-f(4), \\\\ f(-2) & =30=-f(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain of the function. Therefore, $f(x)$ is an odd function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563568 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563682 1069 "q-71088" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563682 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563682 1071 "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{t-3}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{3-t}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt{16-{t}^{2}}\\,i+{2}^{t-2}\\,j+({t}^{2}-4)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563682 1072 #uuid "48d56ec2-9a26-5561-ad14-109677da4c62" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563682 1074 "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\frac{1}{t-3}$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )$, as it  is not defined at $t=3.$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\sqrt{3-t}$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,3)$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=3$ and the function is not defined for $t>3.$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt{16-{t}^{2}}$ is differentiable on $(-4,4)$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=\\pm 4$ and the function is not defined for $|t|>4.$\n\n- The second component ${2}^{t-2}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component ${t}^{2}-4$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(-4,4).$ \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cap (-4,4)=(-4,3).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563633 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563683 1069 "q-127807" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563683 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563683 1071 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous on $[-1,1]?$\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(s) & =|{s}^{2}-1|\\,i+\\frac{s+1}{s}\\,j+2{s}^{2}\\,k \\\\ q(s) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-1)\\,i+({s}^{2}+1)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\,k \\\\ r(s) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\,i-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡s\\,j+\\operatorname{csc}⁡s\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563683 1072 #uuid "48e5a226-dec1-538c-a739-b2da6b892931" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563683 1074 "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.  \n\nLet's consider the functions one by one. \n\n- The function $p(s)$ is **not** continuous on $[-1,1]$ because \nits second component, $\\frac{s+1}{s},$ is undefined at $s=0$ and hence discontinuous on $[-1,1].$\n\n- The function $q(s)$ is **not** continuous on $[-1,1]$ because \nits first component, $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-1)$, is undefined at $s=1$ and hence discontinuous on $[-1,1].$\n\n- The function $r(s)$ is **not** continuous on $[-1,1]$ because \nits third component, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡s$, is undefined at $s=0$ and hence discontinuous on $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the functions are continuous on $[-1,1].$\"" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563684 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563566 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563684 1069 "q-167220" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563684 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563684 1071 "The manager wants to determine the number of wooden tables the company should produce per week to make a weekly profit of $\\$4\\,800.$ The required number of wooden tables $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-360x+4\\,800=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be factored as $\\,$$6\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)=0.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563684 1072 #uuid "497dcd2a-064b-5508-ade6-dffd4f30752c" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563684 1074 "We factor $6{x}^{2}-360x+4\\,800$ using the greatest common factor (GCF).\n\nComputing the GCF, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(6{x}^{2},360x,4\\,800)=6\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-360x+4\\,800 & = \\\\ 6\\cdot ({x}^{2})+6\\cdot (-60x)+6\\cdot (800)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot ({x}^{2})+6\\cdot (-60x)+6\\cdot (800) & = \\\\ 6({x}^{2}-60x+800)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}-60x+800.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563685 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563634 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563685 1069 "q-223686" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563685 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563685 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(2x+3)(x-k)$ is $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563685 1072 #uuid "4c9070fc-7290-592a-a3f4-9617ce57a8a0" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563685 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ we will set our result equal to $\\frac{5}{4}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=(2x+3)(x-k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=k.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2}+k)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{2k}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k-3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2k-3}{4} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 2k-3 & =5 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563617 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563686 1069 "q-223668" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563686 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=-(x+6)(x-4)$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563686 1072 #uuid "4cf4852d-d3c2-5b9a-829d-615cf17e0f68" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563686 1074 "From the equation $y=-(x+6)(x-4),$ we can tell that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-6$ and ${x}_{2}=4.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6+4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-1.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563618 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563687 1069 "q-132581" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563687 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563687 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563687 1072 #uuid "4e141666-84d5-5edd-9a4e-a7cb3e788f33" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563687 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563556 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563688 1069 "q-167219" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563688 1071 "Determine the weekly profit if the company produces $24$ wooden tables in a week.\n\nThe company will earn$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$dollars." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563688 1072 #uuid "527dba5f-2797-5f00-97de-509f725bd58d" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563688 1074 "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-6{x}^{2}+360x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=24$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(24) & =-6(24)^{2}+360(24) \\\\  & =-3\\,456+8\\,640 \\\\  & =5\\,184\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $$5\\,184.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563560 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563689 1069 "q-86543" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563689 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563689 1071 "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563689 1072 #uuid "56172e38-6320-5f34-8b75-aab1067f37e6" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563689 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,\\frac{2}{y}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4) & =⟨-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,\\frac{2}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ in the direction of $∇g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)‖ & =‖⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563604 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563690 1069 "q-35115" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563690 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563690 1071 "Find $f(0)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(0)=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\\,.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563690 1072 #uuid "56eb1f68-e0b0-5d8c-82fe-65522b38bb34" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563690 1074 "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 0}[-g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =f(0)-g(0) \\\\  & =f(0)-{{⟨-1,0,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{g(0)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨-2,-3,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩ & =f(0)-⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩ & =f(0) \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-3,-3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563691 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563552 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563691 1069 "q-71006" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563691 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563691 1071 "Find $f(0)$ given that $f(t)$ is a continuous vector-valued function and $u(t)$ is continuous real-valued function for which $u(0)=3$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563691 1072 #uuid "5dae5e77-95b3-5116-bdbb-e15146f075a1" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563691 1074 "Using the scalar function multiple rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}u(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) \\\\  & =u(0)f(0) \\\\  & =3f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ 3f(0) & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-6,-7,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563692 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563534 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563692 1069 "q-3077" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563692 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563692 1071 "$2\\sqrt{8a{b}^{3}}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{9b}=$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563692 1072 #uuid "60bf46ec-c41d-5bd2-93b6-b056ca651773" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563692 1074 "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{8a{b}^{3}}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{9b} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{8a{b}^{3}\\cdot 9b} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{72a{b}^{4}} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{36{b}^{4}\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{(6{b}^{2})^{2}\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{(6{b}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot |6{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot |6|\\cdot |{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot 6\\cdot |{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 6\\cdot |{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 36\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 36{b}^{2}\\sqrt{2a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563693 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563591 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563693 1069 "q-130211" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563693 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563693 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖,$ if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563693 1072 #uuid "6199f517-7214-5d4f-b510-e7e4d600a748" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563693 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{1-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563608 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563694 1069 "q-18760" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563694 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563694 1071 "Let $(a,b)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $a+b?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.05a-0.2b=0.04 \\\\ 0.12a-1.2b=0.24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563694 1072 #uuid "648c2e1a-c875-5372-ad66-c0b63e6cb405" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563694 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply both equations by $100,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.05a-0.2b=0.04 & |\\times 100 \\\\ 0.12a-1.2b=0.24 & |\\times 100\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5a-20b=4 \\\\ 12a-120b=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$ and divide the second equation by $3,$ we will get a $40b$ term in the first equation that can cancel with the $-40b$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5a-20b=4 & |\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 12a-120b=24 & |\\,÷3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-10a+40b=-8 \\\\ 4a-40b=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $b$-variable and allows us to solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10a+\\cancel{40b} & =-8 \\\\ 4a-\\cancel{40b} & =8 \\\\ (-10a+4a) & =(-8+8) \\\\ -6a & =0 \\\\ a & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $a=0$ into the equation $5a-20b=4$ to solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-20b & =4 \\\\ 5(0)-20b & =4 \\\\ -20b & =4 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{4}{20} \\\\ b & =-0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=0,\\,b=-0.2.$ So,\n\n$$\na+b=0+(-0.2)=-0.2.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563557 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563695 1069 "q-156691" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563695 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563695 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows the graph of $y={log}_{10}⁡x?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563695 1072 #uuid "66bc9e90-0e6b-5349-afc6-a4320dc355fe" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563695 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y={log}_{10}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain is $x>0$, and the range is $-\\infty <y<\\infty .$\n\n- The graph has the vertical asymptote $x=0.$\n\n- The function is an increasing function.\n\n- The graph passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\n- For $x>1$, the graph is positive and grows very slowly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y\\to \\infty $ (slowly) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a52b4fcfb2cc410c894f69771ff450ceef1366048438345d1444e35186d9d76.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563600 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563696 1069 "q-7869" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563696 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563696 1071 "![](images/56/569d6855e7ebdbd7c2e040d7f0200e72e0df71f88991085e3224570587c33767.png)\n\nThe point $A$ with coordinates $(2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ and the point ${A}^{′}$ with coordinates $(6,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax),$ shown above. What is the value of $a?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563696 1072 #uuid "67b6ab53-2529-5d71-90b1-cd110fcf91e4" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563696 1074 "The graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax)$ can be obtained from $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ by a horizontal stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{a}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ that lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ becomes \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{a}\\cdot 2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=(6,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax).$ \n\nTherefore, $a=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563570 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563697 1069 "q-84906" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563697 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563697 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=-(3x+4)(6x-k)$ is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ The value of $k=$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563697 1072 #uuid "684d163d-4d1e-51cd-8879-d5e5b2f089b6" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563697 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we will set our result equal to $-\\frac{1}{3}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=-(3x+4)(6x-k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{k}{6}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{k}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{8}{6}+\\frac{k}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{k-8}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k-8}{12} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ k-8 & =-4 \\\\ k & =-4+8 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563587 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563698 1069 "q-6978" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563698 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563698 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}}{16{x}^{4}-1}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563698 1072 #uuid "69e4acc7-7029-59de-a5d6-0ccfcee72b69" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563698 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}}{16{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{16{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2+\\frac{3}{x})}{(16-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{x}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(2+\\frac{3}{x})}{(16-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{2+0}{16-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{1}{8}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563598 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563699 1069 "q-97905" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563699 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563699 1071 "$\\int -\\frac{15}{|5x+10|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563699 1072 #uuid "6a55ffae-eeb7-54cf-a5ce-c644ed8a6a5a" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563699 1074 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+5 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =(x+2)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int -\\frac{15}{|5x-10|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\int \\frac{15}{5|x+2|\\sqrt{1+(x+2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=-3\\int \\frac{1}{|x+2|\\sqrt{1+(x+2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x+2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-3\\int \\frac{1}{|x+2|\\sqrt{1+(x+2)^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡u+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+2)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563700 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563521 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563700 1069 "q-71799" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563700 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563700 1071 "Which of the following sets are open?\n\nI. $(-4,1)$\n\nII. $(2,4)\\cup (5,6)$\n\nIII. $(0,1)\\cup (7,9]$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563700 1072 #uuid "6e1139bd-70c8-5172-a11e-474495b10da9" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563700 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $(-4,1)$ and $(2,4)\\cup (5,6)$ are open because:\n\n- The boundary points of the set $(-4,1)$ are $-4$, and $1.$ None of them lie in the set.\n\n- The boundary points of the set  $(2,4)\\cup (5,6)$ are $2$, $4$, $5$, and $6.$ None of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other set is not open because $(0,1)\\cup (7,9]$ has four boundary points $0$, $1$, $7$, and $9$, and the point $9$ lies in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563701 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563579 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563701 1069 "q-220354" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563701 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563701 1071 "![](images/bd/bd46cb7a3139ccb19b7f65bab927ef8c56ba075520262649d6fa196cdab8cc81.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a parabola. The $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are $-\\frac{16}{5}$ and $\\frac{5}{2},$ respectively.  Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola.\n\nThe axis of symmetry is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563701 1072 #uuid "6e6642fc-4b0f-571e-8c66-e87cc2512b11" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563701 1074 "We're given that the roots of the parabola are \n ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{16}{5}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{16}{5}+\\frac{5}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{32}{10}+\\frac{25}{10})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{7}{10})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{7}{20}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563524 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563605 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563702 1069 "q-335434" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563702 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563702 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563702 1072 #uuid "701145a1-1f00-509b-9c1b-60626233c2a4" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563702 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1))\\, \\\\ \\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563545 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563703 1069 "q-98150" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563703 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563703 1071 "![](images/65/6583ad253785716c7ead3bb45083a6498fdf45b48c4572a7339b2e8c6f43266a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y=k{log}_{2}⁡(bx).$ Find the value of $k\\cdot b.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563703 1072 #uuid "736b21cf-cbb6-5fc6-8626-c4e49709882d" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563703 1074 "Clearly, $b\\ne 0.$ Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{b}$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{log}_{2}⁡(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =k{log}_{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(\\frac{1}{b},0)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(3,0)$ lies on the curve. Hence\n\n$$\n(3,0)=(\\frac{1}{b},0)\\,⇒\\,b=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(6,2)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{log}_{2}⁡(bx) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}x) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6) & =2 \\\\ k{log}_{2}⁡2 & =2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nk\\cdot b=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563704 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563573 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563704 1069 "q-251806" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563704 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563704 1071 "Consider the two sets of $8$ paired observations in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $16$ | $9$ | $12$ | $10$ | $11$ | $11$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $10$ | $14$ | $8$ | $10$ | $7$ | $8$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=695,$ calculate ${S}_{xy}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563704 1072 #uuid "7487be5e-d3cc-5ed9-9625-a2ce92482f7c" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563704 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of products of deviations from the means can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =16+9+12+10+11+11+6+9=84, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} & =10+14+8+10+7+8+2+4=63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=695.$\n\nFinally, by substituting in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=695-\\frac{1}{8}(84)(63)=695-661.5=33.5.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563525 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563705 1069 "q-106153" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563705 1071 "Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(x+1)(x+5).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563705 1072 #uuid "7a1b4ade-6b6f-5b17-86f4-ce0cd6af2275" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563705 1074 "From the equation $y=(x+1)(x+5),$ we can tell that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=-5.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+(-5)}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-3.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563636 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563706 1069 "q-69736" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563706 1071 "Find ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }[g(t)+2f(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{t})\\,j+{t}^{-2}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =2\\,i+{e}^{-t}\\,j+{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563706 1072 #uuid "7d74654a-36dd-50ce-be7e-dc0c748c4dcb" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563706 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}})\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{t}))\\,j-({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-2})\\,k, \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }2)\\,i-({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{e}^{-t})\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's find the limits one-by-one.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-1}=-1 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{t})=1+{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{t}=1 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-2}={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}=0 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }2=2 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{e}^{-t}=0 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =-1\\,i+1\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-i+j, \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) & =2\\,i+0\\,j+e\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+e\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }[g(t)+2f(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) \\\\  & =(2\\,i+e\\,k)+2(-i+j) \\\\  & =2j+e\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563626 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563707 1069 "q-33465" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563707 1071 "![](images/0c/0c09cf7f047cb341d3e2e2337ac41847d9cf5ff9435d075414a7db092aec3be2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y={log}_{4}⁡(x-d)+c.$ Find the value of $\\frac{d}{c}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563707 1072 #uuid "7ee0988d-03dd-5330-89a0-142478096881" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563707 1074 "In the given graph, the vertical asymptote is $x=4.$ So, the argument of the logarithm function should evaluate to zero at $x=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n4-d=0\\,⇒\\,d=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(8,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡(x-d)+c & =y \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(x-4)+c & =y \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(8-4)+c & =3 \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(4)+c & =3 \\\\ 1+c & =3 \\\\ c & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{c}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563597 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563708 1069 "q-72132" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563708 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563708 1071 "Is the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+8x$ even, odd, or neither?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563708 1072 #uuid "81602105-d969-550d-9191-b0d8d8e1aacf" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563708 1074 "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{3}+8(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne f(x),$ the function is not even.\n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-({x}^{3}+8x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ the function is odd." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563533 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563709 1069 "q-70480" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563709 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563709 1071 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in [0,1]?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(t) & =⟨t-1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ q(t) & =⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}⟩ \\\\ r(t) & =⟨3t+2,\\,|t|,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563709 1072 #uuid "82aa3655-fc19-581e-ac6b-187bbf004ee0" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563709 1074 "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all $t\\in [0,1].$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $t\\in [0,1]$ if and only if each component is differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one by one.\n\n- We differentiate $p(t)$ to find ${p}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${p}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $[0,1],$ we conclude that $p(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $q(t)$ to find ${q}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt[3]{2t-1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{(2t-1)^{2}}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the third component of this vector, $\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{(2t-1)^{2}}},$ is undefined at $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that the function $q(t)$ is not differentiable on $[0,1].$\n\n- Finally, since the deviative of the second component $|t|$ of $r(t)$ is undefined at $t=0,$ we conclude that $r(t)$ is not differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"$p(t)$ only.\"" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563632 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563710 1069 "q-69693" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563710 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows the graph of $y={log}_{2}⁡x?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563710 1072 #uuid "82de78d8-db02-58ba-afa8-a2bcae276094" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563710 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y={log}_{2}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain is $x>0$, and the range is $-\\infty <y<\\infty .$\n\n- The graph has the vertical asymptote $x=0.$\n\n- The function is an increasing function.\n\n- The graph passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\n- For $x>1$, the graph is positive and grows very slowly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y\\to \\infty $ (slowly) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/28/285e69e8c0914643c1fb695d0146a5e83304d74f9e8fbd8efbc2100a7f32bd9e.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563561 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563711 1069 "q-70558" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563711 1071 "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}+4t}{t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j+\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,k$ is continuous." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563711 1072 #uuid "8698245e-3b42-575d-8a24-d13bd09126d2" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563711 1074 "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one-by-one.\n\n- The function $\\frac{{t}^{2}+4t}{t}$ is continuous everywhere except the excluded point $t=0.$\n\n- The function ${t}^{2}-1$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\n- The function $\\frac{1}{t-2}$ is continuous everywhere except the point $t=2.$\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous everywhere except the points $t=0$ and $t=2.$ So the interval of continuity is $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563623 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563712 1069 "q-69728" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563712 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖$, if $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3t \\\\ \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ 5-2t\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563712 1072 #uuid "86cd099b-e0a6-5dec-b63e-d3e60dda2214" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563712 1074 "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) & ={3}^{2}-3(3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}-8}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t) & =5-2(3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563642 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563713 1069 "q-130270" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563713 1071 "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,0,-2)?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563713 1072 #uuid "8889b56a-bb69-56d7-b6a6-42f214a1a3f7" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563713 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,0,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x-yz,\\,2y-xz,\\,2z-xy⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,0,-2) & =⟨2(1)-0(-2),2(0)-1(-2),2(-2)-1(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2,2,-4⟩.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563583 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563714 1069 "q-127725" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563714 1071 "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t),$ if $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+(t+2)^{2}\\,k.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563714 1072 #uuid "8a16b58b-bb85-5099-b69a-a964f563de29" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563714 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j+({lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+2)^{2})k.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t+2)^{2} & =(0+2)^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=j+4\\,k.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563715 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563620 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563715 1069 "q-156742" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563715 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563715 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y={log}_{8}⁡x?$\n\nI. The domain is $x\\ge 0$\n\nII. It has no horizontal asymptotes\n\nIII. It passes through the point $(64,2)$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563715 1072 #uuid "96d6c320-22a1-578b-bc1b-575bf1a1c004" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563715 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y={log}_{8}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/68/6811e57f1259b97448811ac9abe8d6fc7f76a52b62e685f2fa0ec36e290381b4.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, we see that the domain is $x>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $y={log}_{8}⁡x$ has no horizontal asymptote. However, it does have the vertical asymptote $x=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Substituting $x=64$ into the equation of the curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{8}⁡(64) \\\\  & ={log}_{8}⁡({8}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{log}_{8}⁡(8) \\\\  & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563610 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563716 1069 "q-32976" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563716 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖$, if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\,.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563716 1072 #uuid "9a567a48-e070-587a-94df-8a2d7148a763" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563716 1074 "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} & =\\frac{0}{(0+1)^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{″}}{{(2{s}^{2})}^{″}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}]\\,.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $0$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563563 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563717 1069 "q-4701" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563717 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}-9}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563717 1072 #uuid "9b2994c1-1a8d-5ce0-a865-b7991c7a84f8" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563717 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x},\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563622 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563718 1069 "q-4064" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563718 1071 "$3\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{27}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}=$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563718 1072 #uuid "a056777d-2d90-5391-b8e4-3c8ae89bd6c6" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563718 1074 "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{27}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-\\sqrt{{x}^{5}} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{9\\cdot 3}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-\\sqrt{{x}^{4}\\cdot x} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-\\sqrt{{x}^{4}}\\cdot \\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}-3\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{3}-3\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ (3-3)\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ 0\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563546 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563719 1069 "q-34114" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563719 1071 "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563719 1072 #uuid "a8427e64-b547-5ecb-a62e-824aa188b632" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563719 1074 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot (-1)=-2.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563520 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563720 1069 "q-97770" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563720 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563720 1072 #uuid "a95ee677-1642-5975-8b50-8432d56de160" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563720 1074 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-4x & =-({x}^{2}+4x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-((x+2)^{2}-4) \\\\  & =4-(x+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)\\sqrt{4-(x+2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2(x+2)\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+2}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+2}{2})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+2}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{x+2}{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+2}{2})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+2}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{x+2}{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563629 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563721 1069 "q-84895" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563721 1071 "![](images/ce/ce468433e58a99da31fb305428ff478ddb4c91ec6fa260ab1a951979e83ec63c.png)\n\nFind the axis of symmetry for the parabola sketched above." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563721 1072 #uuid "a9694bfd-2888-58c6-a0ef-19d3c4d9b6a1" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563721 1074 "From the graph, we deduce that the roots are ${x}_{1}=-4$ and ${x}_{2}=3.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-4)+3}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563613 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563722 1069 "q-28248" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563722 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563722 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4)?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563722 1072 #uuid "a981d1c6-7401-5c05-a9ed-dfe4b1119a94" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563722 1074 "To plot $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/e5/e595bc1d2c4998c19d1e878bf04856cabb3eef94e665e527449ded66eded6ef1.png)\n\n- Translate it by $4$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4).$\n\n![](images/4b/4b5e2469ce9b19817f67b95a0ac0b402a3d5dec6db92b79f7c7e18331ef267af.png)" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563548 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563723 1069 "q-29322" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563723 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563723 1071 "Simplify $2x\\sqrt{54}+2x\\sqrt{x}-5x\\sqrt{6}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563723 1072 #uuid "aceecb75-92cf-58b4-997d-d31ecad9d81f" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563723 1074 "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\sqrt{54}+2x\\sqrt{x}-5x\\sqrt{6} & = \\\\ 2x\\sqrt{9\\cdot 6}+2x\\sqrt{x}-5x\\sqrt{6} & = \\\\ 2x\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}+2x\\sqrt{x}-5x\\sqrt{6} & = \\\\ 2x\\cdot 3\\cdot \\sqrt{6}+2x\\sqrt{x}-5x\\sqrt{6} & = \\\\ 6x\\sqrt{6}+2x\\sqrt{x}-5x\\sqrt{6} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x\\sqrt{6}+2x\\sqrt{x}-5x\\sqrt{6} & = \\\\ (6x-5x)\\sqrt{6}+2x\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ x\\sqrt{6}+2x\\sqrt{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563724 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563637 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563724 1069 "q-122050" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563724 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563724 1071 "In the right triangle $△JKL,$ $∠J$ is a right angle, $JL=8$ and $KL=12.$ Find $\\operatorname{tan}⁡K.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563724 1072 #uuid "b2b55850-322e-5b17-9a94-b2696bd083f6" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563724 1074 "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/cc/cc33327cc6d071cb6ec9ac0c10d58e57bc35f8d81b326731120cf04c18f58ec1.png)\n\nRecall the definition of tangent: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡K=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}=\\frac{JL}{JK}=\\frac{8}{JK}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $JK.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}JK & =\\sqrt{K{L}^{2}-J{L}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{12}^{2}-{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144-64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡K & =\\frac{8}{4\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563725 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563528 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563725 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563635 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563725 1069 "q-167218" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563725 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563725 1071 "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $100a+15b=4\\,800.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563725 1072 #uuid "b42bde79-43e7-5e0d-aa7f-ed2b4b36837d" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563725 1074 "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}b=270-15a \\\\ 100a+15b=4\\,800\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=270-15a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100a+15b & =4\\,800 \\\\ 100a+15(270-15a) & =4\\,800 \\\\ 100a+4\\,050-225a & =4\\,800 \\\\ -125a+4\\,050 & =4\\,800 \\\\ -125a & =750 \\\\ a & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-6$ into $b=270-15a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =270-15(-6) \\\\ b & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-6,\\,b=360.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563602 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563726 1069 "q-18691" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563726 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563726 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3.6v+21.6=0 \\\\ 3v+4w+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563726 1072 #uuid "b521b5c1-2f07-5bb3-9c22-0775852024a2" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563726 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the top equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3.6v+21.6=0 & |\\,\\times 10 \\\\ 3v+4w+2=0 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}36v+216=0 \\\\ 3v+4w+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can easily solve for $v$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36v+216 & =0 \\\\ 36v & =-216 \\\\ v & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the value of $w,$ we can substitute $v=-6$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3v+4w+2 & =0 \\\\ 3(-6)+4w+2 & =0 \\\\ -18+4w+2 & =0 \\\\ 4w & =16 \\\\ w & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $v=-6,\\,w=4.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563535 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563727 1069 "q-69730" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563727 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563727 1071 "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563727 1072 #uuid "b9ad6ae2-908f-5e67-9c92-f2b932e8cd1f" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563727 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}{t}^{2} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{0} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the third component is a special limit.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}1 \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2(0)-1}{{0}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563574 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563728 1069 "q-311909" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563728 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563728 1071 "| **$x$** | $8$ | $10$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$f(x)$** | $18$ | $12$ | $12$ |\n\nThe graph of a parabola $y=f(x)$ passes through the points given by their $(x,y)$-coordinates in the table above. What is the $x$-coordinate of the parabola's vertex?\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563728 1072 #uuid "bac157c9-805d-56d0-9e89-ae7a2f0ad4e6" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563728 1074 "Notice that the values of $f(x)$ at the points $x=10$ and $x=16$ are equal.\n\n$$\nf(10)=f(16)=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola's axis of symmetry must be equidistant from the lines $x=10$ and $x=16.$ We can find this by taking the average of these two values.\n\nIf the parabola's vertex is $(h,k),$ then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{10+16}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{2} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563562 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563729 1069 "q-130321" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563729 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563729 1071 "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\times g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨6-{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}-6,\\,\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}⟩, \\\\ g(t) & =⟨5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t),\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563729 1072 #uuid "bd1d10fc-2d48-5d8e-aa22-57b49e0e6a49" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563729 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,(6-{t}^{3}),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,({t}^{2}-6),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6-{2}^{3},{2}^{2}-6,\\frac{16}{{2}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩ \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,(5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2),\\sqrt{6-2},2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\times g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)\\times {lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩\\times ⟨5,2,4⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2)\\,i-((-2)\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 2)\\,j+((-2)\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-2))\\,k \\\\  & =-12\\,i+18\\,j+6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-12,18,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563628 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563730 1069 "q-71214" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563730 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563730 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563730 1072 #uuid "bd4791a4-a5ea-5e12-b1c8-f45a53372bd0" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563730 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})} & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})}(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z=2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563532 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563731 1069 "q-141498" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563731 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563731 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{6{a}^{3}b}\\cdot \\sqrt{15abc}$ given that $a,b,c\\ge 0.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563731 1072 #uuid "bd6c8038-fcc2-5fa5-ad44-54f20efc3514" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563731 1074 "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6{a}^{3}b}\\cdot \\sqrt{15abc} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{6{a}^{3}b\\cdot 15abc} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{90{a}^{4}{b}^{2}c} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9{a}^{4}{b}^{2}\\cdot 10c} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(3{a}^{2}b)^{2}\\cdot 10c} & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\cdot |b|\\cdot \\sqrt{10c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have that $|{a}^{2}|={a}^{2}$ and $|b|=b.$  Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\cdot |b|\\cdot \\sqrt{10c} & = \\\\ 3{a}^{2}b\\sqrt{10c} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563732 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563577 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563732 1069 "q-105199" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563732 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563732 1071 "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563732 1072 #uuid "c391d9c3-0c76-5e6f-b0c3-d6b4ee2c37cb" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563732 1074 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 2=6.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563733 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563643 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563733 1069 "q-101729" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563733 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563733 1071 "The table below lists values of $f(x)$ at distinct $x$-values. Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[3p,5]$ is $-2,$ find the value of $p.$ \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-10\\,$ | $\\,-7\\,$ | $\\,3p\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,12\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ | $\\,11\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,-16\\,$ | $\\,-8\\,$ |" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563733 1072 #uuid "c6acc05f-c544-5e0c-918b-0d7218132aa6" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563733 1074 "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(5)-f(3p)}{5-3p} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8-11}{5-3p} \\\\  & =\\frac{-19}{5-3p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=-2$ on the interval $[3p,5].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{-19}{5-3p}=-2.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-19}{5-3p} & =-2 \\\\ -19 & =-2\\cdot (5-3p) \\\\ -19 & =-10+6p \\\\ -19+10 & =6p \\\\ -9 & =6p \\\\ \\frac{-9}{6} & =\\frac{6p}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & =p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $p=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563734 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563603 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563734 1069 "q-105190" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563734 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563734 1071 "Which of the following sets are open?\n\nI. $[0,15]$\n\nII. $(-3,5]\\cup [6,8)$\n\nIII. $(1,6)$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563734 1072 #uuid "c7bf2d22-67db-5bfb-9d76-d999020db50d" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563734 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $(1,6)$ is open. The boundary points are $1$, and $6.$ None of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other sets are not open because:\n\n- $[0,15]$ has two boundary points $0$, and $15$, and both of them lie in the set.\n\n- $(-3,5]\\cup [6,8)$ has four boundary points $-3$, $5$, $6$, and $8$, and the points $5$ and $6$ lie in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563735 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563549 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563735 1069 "q-84902" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563735 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563735 1071 "Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(6x-1)(3x+1).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563735 1072 #uuid "cd5e69ab-4017-5036-a080-1d135b13f5a0" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563735 1074 "From the equation $y=(6x-1)(3x+1),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{12}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563736 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563611 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563736 1069 "q-29319" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563736 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563736 1071 "Simplify $3\\sqrt[3]{y}-4\\sqrt{y}-7\\sqrt[3]{y}+2\\sqrt{y}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563736 1072 #uuid "ce532f1b-ce31-55ab-8ca6-180ef8818c5e" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563736 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{y}$ terms together, and the two $\\sqrt{y}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt[3]{y}-4\\sqrt{y}-7\\sqrt[3]{y}+2\\sqrt{y} & = \\\\ (-4+2)\\sqrt{y}+(3-7)\\sqrt[3]{y} & = \\\\ -2\\sqrt{y}-4\\sqrt[3]{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot combine the $\\sqrt[3]{y}$ and $\\sqrt{y}$ terms." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563595 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563737 1069 "q-71094" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563737 1071 "Find the interval where the function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & ={t}^{2}+2 \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt[3]{t-3}\\,i+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563737 1072 #uuid "cff5ef5d-ae0d-5005-af50-0efa2caca50a" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563737 1074 "The function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where both $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. The vector function $g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where its components are differentiable.  \n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. \n\nFor the real-valued function $u(t):$  \n\n- We observe that $u(t)$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component, $\\sqrt[3]{t-3}$, is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )$ as its derivative is not defined at $t=3.$\n\n- The second and third components, ${cos}^{2}⁡t$ and $\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}},$ are differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\nTherefore, the intersection of the domains of all three components of  $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$  \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563586 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563738 1069 "q-130202" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563738 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563738 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)$, if $f(s)=⟨\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,{s}^{3}-5⟩.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563738 1072 #uuid "d22af4f5-52e4-5523-b72f-519be01c5f87" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563738 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,{lim}_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5)⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}} & =\\frac{5}{{e}^{2-2}}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) & =3{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5) & ={2}^{3}-5=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨5,3,3⟩.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563531 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563739 1069 "q-311956" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563739 1071 "| **$x$** | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$f(x)$** | $-10$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nThe graph of a parabola $y=f(x)$ passes through the points given by their $(x,y)$-coordinates in the table above. The parabola opens downward. What is the $x$-coordinate of the parabola's vertex?\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563739 1072 #uuid "d2b05f92-b206-55b0-8a81-97fed9a6e7ee" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563739 1074 "Notice that the roots of the parabola are given. They are \n\n$$\nx=-6\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nIf the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k),$ then by the average of the roots formula, $h$ is simply the average of the two roots. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{-6+(-3)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563541 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563740 1069 "q-311977" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563740 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563740 1071 "| **$x$** | $22$ | $25$ | $29$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$f(x)$** | $7$ | $7$ | $1$ |\n\nThe graph of a parabola $y=f(x)$ passes through the points given by their $(x,y)$-coordinates in the table above. What is the $x$-coordinate of the parabola's vertex?\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563740 1072 #uuid "d3719257-12a3-5f28-b262-f0a159519b77" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563740 1074 "Notice that the values of $f(x)$ at the points $x=22$ and $x=25$ are equal.\n\n$$\nf(22)=f(25)=7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola's axis of symmetry must be equidistant from the lines $x=22$ and $x=25.$ We can find this by taking the average of these two values.\n\nIf the parabola's vertex is $(h,k),$ then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{22+25}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{47}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563612 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563741 1069 "q-86430" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563741 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563741 1071 "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-2,2).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563741 1072 #uuid "d5075c32-f624-572f-a2d3-78616466d14e" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563741 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨-2,2⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨16{x}^{3}-8xy,\\,2y-4{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-1,1) & =⟨16(-1)^{3}-8(-1)(1),\\,2(1)-4(-1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇f(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})-2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563630 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563742 1069 "q-100250" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563742 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563742 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563742 1072 #uuid "d591e3b7-0993-5fb9-b7f0-ff97c33bf559" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563742 1074 "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}3x}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}3x & =3{lim}_{x\\to 2}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule in the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5} & =\\sqrt[3]{{lim}_{x\\to 2}(2{x}^{4}-5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{2(2)^{4}-5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}} & =\\frac{6}{3}=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563743 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563543 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563743 1069 "q-32701" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563743 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563743 1071 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨t\\sqrt{t},\\,1-{t}^{2},\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563743 1072 #uuid "d69b9419-ecda-5adb-b076-9a4d08de1698" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563743 1074 "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all  $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi ).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $(\\pi ,2\\pi )$ if and only if each of its component functions is differentiable on that interval. \n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $f(t)$ to find ${f}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${f}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )$, we conclude that $f(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $g(t)$ to find ${g}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\sqrt{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{1/2},\\,-2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t},\\,-2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of  ${g}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )$, we have that $g(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $h(t)$ to find ${h}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,2,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of  ${h}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )$, we conclude that $h(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is: \n\n$\\,$ All the functions are differentiable on $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563744 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563606 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563744 1069 "q-97777" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563744 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563744 1071 "$\\int \\frac{3}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563744 1072 #uuid "d6b27f7b-e94e-5d63-abcb-feb8d8bed4dd" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563744 1074 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-6x & =-({x}^{2}+6x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) \\\\  & =-((x+3)^{2}-9) \\\\  & =9-(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{(x+3)\\sqrt{9-{(x+3)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+3}{3})\\sqrt{9-9{(\\frac{x+3}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+3}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{x+3}{3}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+3}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563539 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563745 1069 "q-156696" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563745 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563745 1071 "What's the range of $f(x)={log}_{10}⁡x?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563745 1072 #uuid "d6e79644-f01f-5d41-959c-0424a55445f9" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563745 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{10}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/46/460a26a36316db034c93017f45aba301b1a39146b1dcb60b3024d3a20a824314.png)\n\nThe domain of $y={log}_{10}⁡x$ is $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ and the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All real numbers.\"" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563571 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563746 1069 "q-84903" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563746 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563746 1071 "Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(7-5x)(x+1).$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563746 1072 #uuid "da0e0865-56b2-565d-ac33-deaee1268cf4" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563746 1074 "From the equation $y=(7-5x)(x+1),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{5}$ and ${x}_{2}=-1.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{5}-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{5}-\\frac{5}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{5}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563747 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563584 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563747 1069 "q-156700" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563747 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563747 1071 "What's the domain of $y={log}_{8}⁡x?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563747 1072 #uuid "db1e03ac-5baa-5855-b6ca-f2c610df9446" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563747 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{8}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/8e/8e38a8edb560a0ddaa2e7802048ea0285e7bf3e72325de0a48f2672d9e81112c.png)\n\nThe domain of $y={log}_{8}⁡x$ is $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ and the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x>0.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563551 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563748 1069 "q-127809" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563748 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563748 1071 "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,2{e}^{t-1},\\,\\sqrt{5-t}⟩$ is continuous." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563748 1072 #uuid "dd43e1d4-a6c0-5d8f-9dc6-6b6b8e30eb50" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563748 1074 "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one by one.\n\n- The function $t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\n- The function $2{e}^{t-1}$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\n- The function $\\sqrt{5-t}$ is continuous for $t\\le 5.$\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous for $t\\le 5.$ So the interval of continuity is $(-\\infty ,5].$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563523 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563749 1069 "q-34639" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563749 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563749 1071 "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, ${a}_{3}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563749 1072 #uuid "ddfb229d-f01d-542c-8a6c-f2e9a5996154" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563749 1074 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=18 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -8 & 18 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{3}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=3.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(3)=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=9.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{a}_{1}+6{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 9\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=9\\cdot 6\\cdot 3=162.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563750 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563542 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563750 1069 "q-130258" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563750 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563750 1071 "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2}$ at the point $(-1,3)$ in the direction of the vector $v=i+j.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563750 1072 #uuid "df6440e4-6b40-5b4e-87c9-62e58eb89d0e" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563750 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2x-6y)\\,i+(2y-6x)j \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,3) & =(2(-1)-6(3))\\,i+(2(3)-6(-1))j \\\\  & =-20\\,i+12\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,3) & =∇\\,f(-1,3)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(-20\\,i+12\\,j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{-20(1)+12(1)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563751 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563631 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563751 1069 "q-130317" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563751 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563751 1071 "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t-2 \\\\ \\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563751 1072 #uuid "e00ec48a-b851-58e1-a999-f80b28436a4f" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563751 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}3{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{0} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+9}{0+3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t-2) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{2}^{t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0-2 \\\\ \\frac{3(0)-2}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{0}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3(-2)+0(2)+3(1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563538 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563752 1069 "q-130214" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563752 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563752 1071 "Find the limit $\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}‖f(t)‖,$ if $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+(t+2)^{2}\\,k.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563752 1072 #uuid "e2503fc5-32a5-54fb-be4e-1b9923b33c22" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563752 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j+({lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+2)^{2})k.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t+2)^{2} & =(0+2)^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=j+4\\,k.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖j+4\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563554 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563753 1069 "q-161587" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563753 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563753 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<1-x\\}.\n$$\n\nThe boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563753 1072 #uuid "e603e7ce-cd31-56e2-8f70-d962ee5aba80" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563753 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5151df3c5b84ffaca9aa16b401cd9364fea10a240d39c9c28d815b36e417b16d.png)\n\n \n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the line $y=1-x.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y=1-x\\,\\}.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563754 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563596 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563754 1069 "q-130205" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563754 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563754 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s),$ if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563754 1072 #uuid "e7a9644a-6597-5c1c-8581-2dcd08c08372" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563754 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}.\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{{e}^{1-1}}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1} \\\\  & ={6}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563755 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563593 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563755 1069 "q-335025" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563755 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563755 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563755 1072 #uuid "eaac4ca3-6936-5237-9e98-3d7aa72b5385" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563755 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563756 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563576 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563756 1069 "q-91348" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563756 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563756 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation ${x}^{7}=-\\frac{1}{128}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563756 1072 #uuid "ee997e26-088f-52b1-8913-d296b8992e92" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563756 1074 "We can solve this equation by taking the $7$th root of each side and solving for $x.$ Since $n=7$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{7} & =-\\frac{1}{128} \\\\ \\sqrt[7]{{x}^{7}} & =\\sqrt[7]{(-\\frac{1}{128})} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[7]{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{7}} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563757 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563589 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563757 1069 "q-71089" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563757 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563757 1071 "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\sqrt[3]{t}\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j+(5+t)\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563757 1072 #uuid "ef36aafd-d427-51c9-9cd0-af8bbd24e5b8" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563757 1074 "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt[3]{t}$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$, as its derivative is not defined at  $t=0.$\n\n- The second component ${e}^{2t}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $(5+t)$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)$ is differentiable on $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is \n\n$$\n[(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )]\\cap (-1,\\infty )=(-1,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563581 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563758 1069 "q-127893" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563758 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563758 1071 "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =|t-1{|}^{3}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}\\,i+{e}^{\\sqrt{t-1}}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563758 1072 #uuid "ef76f5ac-70f5-5f95-86dd-2e2b577b8988" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563758 1074 "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $|t-1{|}^{3}$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)$ is differentiable over $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(1,\\infty ).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}$ is differentiable over $(-2,2).$\n\n- The second component ${e}^{\\sqrt{t-1}}$ is differentiable for $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component ${t}^{3}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(1,2).$ \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is\n\n$$\n(1,\\infty )\\cap (1,2)=(1,2).\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563569 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563759 1069 "q-167221" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563759 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563759 1071 "Find the minimum number of wooden tables the company needs to produce per week to make a weekly profit of $\\$4\\,800.$\n\nThe company needs to produce$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ wooden tables per week." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563759 1072 #uuid "f01f8f3f-d44f-517a-a672-79bef06856d4" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563759 1074 "From part 5, the required number of wooden tables $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cdot ({x}^{2}-60x+800)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $6,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-60x+800=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(800)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-60).$ The required factors are $(-20)$ and $(-40).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-60x=-20x-40x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-60x+800 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-20x-40x+800 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-20x-40x+800 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-20x)+(-40x+800) & =0 \\\\ x(x-20)-40(x-20) & =0 \\\\ (x-20)(x-40)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=20$ or $x=40.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the company needs to produce $20$ wooden tables per week to make a profit of $$4\\,800.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563567 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563760 1069 "q-83552" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563760 1071 "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=2i+j.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563760 1072 #uuid "f7872fd6-9c54-5e48-b08b-71d8ca0a2063" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563760 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(2i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+\\frac{y}{x})\\,i+(\\frac{x}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{1}{1})\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+j)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563619 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563761 1069 "q-167222" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563761 1071 "The company wants to increase its output by implementing a new production model. The number of wooden tables ${a}_{n}$ produced in the $n$th week since the implementation of the new model is given by the arithmetic sequence\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+4,\\,{a}_{1}=12,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nWhat is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence?\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563761 1072 #uuid "f85c7e50-3cac-5133-ab86-e4e54ecc65fa" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563761 1074 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=12,$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+4 \\\\  & =12+4 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =16-12 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute ${a}_{1}=12$ and $d=4$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =12+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =12+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=4n+8,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563762 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563526 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563762 1069 "q-30510" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563762 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563762 1071 "![](images/6e/6ecca5e6b96cd7e0de0510abf26b3fe80c6f33322460b9bf4448f4b9c3eb8442.png)\n\nFind the axis of symmetry for the parabola sketched above." 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563762 1072 #uuid "f8e7577e-52b0-551a-a683-aebcdeb0ba63" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563762 1074 "From the graph, we deduce that the roots are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=2.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-1)+2}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563763 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563641 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563763 1069 "q-83591" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563763 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563763 1071 "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563763 1072 #uuid "ffacb628-f280-51e0-9811-c0f525cc08ac" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563763 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+4x-\\frac{1}{x})i-x{sec}^{2}⁡y\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{1})i-1\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-4\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $-4\\,i-2\\,j.$" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563764 1058 #edb/ref 17592186563785 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563764 1062 #uuid "0b859571-5266-5412-bce5-e8cf5c6397c7" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563764 1063 #edb/ref 17592186563755 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563765 1058 #edb/ref 17592186563792 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563765 1062 #uuid "0e849385-5c84-5ebb-9c6a-2fbbf98e75fc" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563765 1063 #edb/ref 17592186362318 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563766 1058 #edb/ref 17592186563774 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563766 1062 #uuid "19daaae6-6d2a-56f2-a06f-7650bdc33cd8" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563766 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078737 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563767 1062 #uuid "1bd75efd-e9f9-5e75-ad2e-b07553799349" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563767 1063 #edb/ref 17592186563665 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563768 1062 #uuid "1befb3fc-18d4-5289-828d-35a76c78d3b1" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563768 1063 #edb/ref 17592186563644 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563769 1062 #uuid "227deb9f-a9be-5caf-9b21-75db4f696e28" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563769 1063 #edb/ref 17592186563651 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563770 1062 #uuid "25b65737-ade2-56c9-a926-c3e2f2a2500e" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563770 1063 #edb/ref 17592186563675 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563771 1062 #uuid "270ae88f-7164-59c9-b5e2-e9c6bfa91401" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563771 1063 #edb/ref 17592186563761 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563772 1058 #edb/ref 17592186563794 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563772 1062 #uuid "2e1eff63-f8c6-5d02-9662-7565ab1c3899" 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17592186563772 1063 #edb/ref 17592186563742 13194139536064 17592186045417 true] [17